INSERT INTO questions (idx, dataset, example_id, prompt_id, source, subject, question_number, prompt, question, choice_a, choice_b, choice_c, choice_d, answer, gold_passage, gold_idx, generated, subtopic)
VALUES
('55', 'mbe', 'mbe_0055', 'prompt_0338', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '12', '', 'The reward notice in the newspaper constituted an:', 'Bilateral contract.', 'Offer to negotiate.', 'Unilateral contract.', 'Unilateral contract which be- came a bilateral contract.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('550', 'mbe', 'mbe_0550', 'prompt_0864', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '73', '', 'Under which category or categories of class action does Joe''s class action fit?', '"Prejudice" class action [Rule 23(b)(1)].', '"Injunctive" class action [Rule 23(b)(2)].', '"Damage" class action [Rule 23(b)(3)].', 'Both "Prejudice" and "Damage" class actions.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('551', 'mbe', 'mbe_0551', 'prompt_0865', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '74', '', 'Which statement best defines the notice requirement applicable to Joe''s class action?', 'The best notice practicable under the circumstances.', 'The best notice practicable under the circumstances, including individual notice to each class member. 96 CIVIL PROCEDURE Q', 'The best notice practicable under the circumstances, including individual notice to all class members who can be identified through reasonable effort.', 'Publication of the class action notice in newspapers of wide circulation.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('552', 'mbe', 'mbe_0552', 'prompt_0866', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '76', '', 'Joe learns that mailing notice to the class will cost $300,000. Joe has rep- resented to the court that he has no more than $10,000 to pay for notice to the class. Which of the following best describes how a court will handle this situation?', 'Joe will have to pay the entire $300,000 himself.', 'The court will order I.B.N. to pay the $280,000 if it determines, after a preliminary hearing, that Joe is likely to win the class ac- tion on the merits.', 'The court will find Joe''s status as class attorney makes him an adequate class representative because it will save the class the cost of hiring a separate attor- ney.', 'The court will find Joe''s status as class attorney to be irrelevant to whether Joe will adequately represent the class. 97 CIVIL PROCEDURE Questions 77 - 79 are based on the following facts: Paul, an Oregonian, and Don, a Californian, were each independent truckers who were driving their respective trucks on a two-lane highway and were approaching each other from opposite directions. Their side-mir- rors struck each other, causing each truck to swerve off the highway killing each driver. Paula,', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('553', 'mbe', 'mbe_0553', 'prompt_0867', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '77', '', 'A year before he died, Don thought it would be prudent to draft a will. Don asked his accountant to review Don''s assets and draft a report so that Don would know the extent, nature and worth of his estate. Paula served a request to produce that report. Donna objected. Which of the following is the strongest objection Donna could make?', 'The report is irrelevant.', 'The report contains confidential information.', 'The report is protected by the work product immunity.', 'The report is protected by F.R. C P. 26(b)(4) [facts and opinions of experts].', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('554', 'mbe', 'mbe_0554', 'prompt_0868', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '78', '', 'Donna sought to depose Eggbert, an expert in accident reconstruction, who has been identified by Paula, pursuant to disclosure requirements, as a wit- ness she has retained to provide expert testimony in the case. Donna wants to depose Eggbert and has served upon him a notice of deposition. Donna has not yet served any interrogatories on Paula, nor has she received any reports prepared by Eggbert on his proposed trial testimony. Paula moved for a pro- tective order to prevent Donna from proceeding with Eggbert''s deposition. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The protective order should be granted because Donna has not yet received any reports prepared by Eggbert regarding his pro- posed trial testimony.', 'The protective order should be granted because Donna has not yet served, and received answers to, interrogatories regarding Eggbert''s proposed trial testi- mony. 98 CIVIL PROCEDURE Q 0 0< c', 'Donna''s motion should be de- nied because, when there is a conflict in the evidence, the jury should resolve that conflict, not the court.', 'The protective order should be denied.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('555', 'mbe', 'mbe_0555', 'prompt_0869', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '79', '', 'At trial, Paula''s only evidence on liabil- ity is the expert testimony of Eggbert who testified that, based upon the cir- cumstantial evidence he viewed at the scene of the accident, including skid marks, the position of the trucks and the condition of the wreckage, it was his opinion that Don''s truck could well have veered into Paul''s lane. On cross- examination, Eggbert also testified that it was equally possible that Paul''s truck could have veered into Don''s lane. Be- fore Paula had an opportunity to put on her damage evidence, Donna moved for judgment as a matter of law on the issue of causation. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'Donna''s motion is premature and, therefore, should not be granted because Paula has not yet put on her whole case.', 'The court should deny the mo- tion because Willy is not a party nor in the custody nor a person in the custody or under the legal control of a party.', 'The court should grant the mo- tion because the condition of Willy''s eyesight is relevant to the case.', 'The court should deny the mo- tion because Willy''s ex-wife is biased.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('556', 'mbe', 'mbe_0556', 'prompt_0870', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '80', '', 'Acme moved, under F.R.C.P. 35(a), to compel an eye examination of Willy, submitting an affidavit from Willy''s ex-wife in which she affirmed that Willy suffers from serious night blindness. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court should grant the mo- tion because Del is a person who is under the control of Acme, Inc., a party defendant.', 'The court should deny the motion because Pam has not shown that Del''s nervous system and mental state are "in controversy."', 'The court should grant the mo- tion because Del''s ability to drive a vehicle safely is centrally relevant to the case. 100 CIVIL PROCEDURE —co 0 0 < CP', 'The court should deny the mo- tion because the affidavit of Pam''s attorney is not based upon personal knowledge.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('557', 'mbe', 'mbe_0557', 'prompt_0871', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '82', '', 'Pam moved for summary judgment submitting in support of her motion the affidavit of Willy that states, under oath, what he is prepared to testify to at trial (as set forth in the base facts above). In response, Acme, Inc. only submitted its answer, in which it denied that Del ran the red light and alleged that Pam did. Which statement most correctly expresses how the court should rule on Pam''s summary judgment motion?', 'The motion should be denied because Pam did not meet her movant''s burden.', 'The motion should be granted because Acme, Inc. did not meet its respondent''s burden.', 'The motion should be denied because there is a two-sided fact issue regarding causation which should go to the jury.', 'The motion should be granted because courts never grant offen- sive summary judgment motions based on affidavit testimony.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('558', 'mbe', 'mbe_0558', 'prompt_0872', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '83', '', 'Assume, for purposes of the remainder of this series of questions, that Pam''s summary judgment motion in Question 82 was denied. At trial, Pam''s liability case consists of (1) Willy ''s testimony as described in the base facts above and (2) the testimony of two bishops (newly discovered by Pam) who swear that they saw Del run the red light. Acme, Inc.''s rebuttal case consists of one witness, a twice-convicted perjurer, who swears that he saw Pam run the red light. After the jury returned a verdict in favor of Acme, Inc., Pam moved for a judgment as a matter of law in her favor and, in the alternative, for a new trial. Which of the following statements most correctly expresses how the court should rule on Pam''s motion for judgment as a matter of law?', 'Pam''s motion should be denied because an offensive motion for judgment as a matter of law can never be granted where the mov- ant has offered oral testimony, since credibility is always a jury issue.', 'Pam''s motion should be granted because Pam met her burden of production and Acme, Inc. did not.', 'Pam''s motion should be denied because Pam did not make a mo- tion for a judgment as a matter of law before the close of all the evidence. CIVIL PROCEDURE 101', 'The reversal was proper because ed because Pam''s case "over- Juror Joan''s affidavit was inad- whelmed" Acme''s case. missible.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('559', 'mbe', 'mbe_0559', 'prompt_0873', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '85', '', 'Beers made a pre-answer motion to dis- miss Pam''s suit on the ground that the court lacked subject matter jurisdiction over her claim. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court has diversity jurisdic- tion.', 'The court does not have diversity jurisdiction because Beers is also a Nevada citizen, like Pam, since it "does business" in Nevada by soliciting business in Nevada.', 'The court does not have diversity jurisdiction because Pam cannot prove to a legal certainty that she will recover the full $75,000 in pain and suffering damages.', 'The court does not have diver- sity jurisdiction because Darren is also a California citizen like Beers.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('56', 'mbe', 'mbe_0056', 'prompt_0339', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '13', '', 'If Annie sues to recover the $250 re- ward, she will: CONTRACTS •', 'Succeed.', 'Succeed, since a contract implied by law was formed.', 'Not succeed, because there was no bargained for exchange.', 'Not succeed. m CONTRACTS Questions 14 -15 are based on the following facts: Sam had always wanted a Honda motor- cycle. Keith, Sam''s friend, had recently re- stored his 1973 Honda motorcycle. On Sep- tember 1, Sam approached Keith and prom- ised to pay Keith $2,500 on September 15 for Keith''s Honda motorcycle. It was agreed that the motorcycle would be delivered on September 9th. The motorcycle was deliv- ered on September 9th as promised by Keith. On September 10th, Sam repudiated.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('560', 'mbe', 'mbe_0560', 'prompt_0874', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '86', '', 'Assume, for purposes of the remain- der of this series of questions, that the court denied Beers'' pre-answer motion in Question 85. Beers then answered Pam''s complaint, denying, in part, (1) that Darren was drunk when he left the bar and (2) that Darren caused the col- lision. Beers'' answer also affirmatively alleged, as affirmative defenses, (a) that Pam''s negligence caused the collision and (b) that the court does not have personal jurisdiction over Beers. Beers then moved to dismiss Pam''s complaint for lack of personal jurisdiction. As- sume Nevada has a long arm statute which limits tort actions in Nevada courts against non-residents to actions arising out of tortious conduct (not just injury) occurring in Nevada. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court can exercise personal jurisdiction over Beers because it solicited business in Nevada. CIVIL PROCEDURE 103', 'The court cannot exercise per- sonal jurisdiction over Beers because Beers serves customers (including Darren) exclusively in California.', 'The court cannot exercise per- sonal jurisdiction over Beers because Pam''s action arises out of tortious conduct occurring in California.', 'The court can exercise per- sonal jurisdiction because Beers waived its personal jurisdiction defense.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('561', 'mbe', 'mbe_0561', 'prompt_0875', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '87', '', 'Assume, for purposes of the remainder of this series of questions, that the court denied Beers'' motion in Question 86. Beers then abandoned defense of the suit, ignoring all discovery orders. The court finally entered a default judg- ment for Pam for $30,000 on July 1, 1992. Two months later, Pam brought another suit against Beers arising out of the same auto collision with Darren, demanding $80,000 in damages for injury to her Jaguar automobile. Beers moved to dismiss Pam''s suit based upon the July 1, 1992 judgment in Pam''s previous suit against Beers. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court should grant the mo- tion.', 'The court should not grant the motion because the 7/1/92 judg- ment was not "on the merits."', 'The court should not grant the motion because Pam''s second suit against Beers asserts a differ- ent right than her previous suit.', 'The court should not grant the motion because the 7/1/92 judgment is not "valid" since that judgment was for less than $75,000.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('562', 'mbe', 'mbe_0562', 'prompt_0876', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '88', '', 'Assume, for purposes of the remainder of this series of questions, that the court granted Beers'' motion in Question 87 and entered judgment for Beers on Sep- tember 1, 1992. Pam filed suit against Darren in a federal district court in Cali- fornia demanding $80,000 in damages for injury to her Jaguar automobile, serving him personally while he was home visiting his parents in Irvine. Darren made a pre-answer motion to dismiss Pam''s complaint on the ground that the court lacked personal jurisdic- tion over him. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court should deny Darren''s motion because Pam''s cause of action arose out of Darren''s drinking in California.', 'The court should grant Darren''s motion because the collision arose directly out of Darren''s driving in Nevada. 104 CIVIL PROCEDURE', 'The court should grant Darren''s motion because most of the evi- dence relating to the cause of the collision, including witnesses, is located in Nevada.', 'The court should deny Darren''s motion because Darren was served in California.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('563', 'mbe', 'mbe_0563', 'prompt_0877', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '88', '', 'Darren moved to dismiss Pam''s suit on the ground that the court lacked subject matter jurisdiction. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'Darren''s motion should be de- nied because Darren is a Califor- nia citizen and Pam is a Nevada citizen.', 'Darren''s motion should be grant- ed because Darren is a Nevada citizen like Pam.', 'Darren''s motion should be grant- ed because Darren is a citizen of the forum state, California.', 'Darren''s motion should be de- nied because he waived his sub- ject matter jurisdiction defense.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('564', 'mbe', 'mbe_0564', 'prompt_0878', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '90', '', 'Assume, for purposes of the remainder of this series of questions, that the court denied Darren''s motion in Question 89. Darren moved to dismiss Pam''s action based upon the September 1, 1992 judg- ment in the second Pam v. Beers suit. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court should deny Darren''s motion because neither res judi- cata nor any joinder rule required Pam to join Darren as a co-defen- dant alongside Beers.', 'The court should deny Darren''s motion because Darren waived his res judicata defense by failing to include it in his pre-answer motion.', 'The court should grant Darren''s motion because Pam should not be allowed to "hedge her bets" by suing a series of defendants, one-by-one, on the same prop- erty claim until the supply of defendants runs out.', 'The court should deny Darren''s motion because the 9/1/92 judg- ment was not "on the merits." CIVIL PROCEDURE 105', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('565', 'mbe', 'mbe_0565', 'prompt_0879', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '90', '', 'Pam moved for partial summary judgment, based on the July 1, 1992 judgment in Pam''s favor in the first Pam v. Beers suit, on the issue of Darren''s liability for causing the collision. Which of the following statements is not cor- rect?', 'Pam cannot invoke collateral es- toppel because the Due Process clause prevents Darren from being bound by the 7/1/92 judg- ment.', 'Pam cannot invoke collateral estoppel because no facts were actually determined in the first Pam v. Beers suit.', 'Pam cannot invoke collateral estoppel because the 7/1/92 judg- ment was not "on the merits."', 'The mutuality of estoppel rule is inapplicable to resolve the is- sue whether Pam can invoke the benefits of collateral estoppel. Questions 92 - 96 are based on the following facts: Jacques and Jill are graduate students at a private university in Pomona, California. Jacques is a French citizen. Prior to her com- ing to California to study, Jill lived with her parents in Phoenix, Arizona. Jill, however, left on very bad terms and vowed never to re- turn to her parents'' home in Arizona. Jacques a', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('566', 'mbe', 'mbe_0566', 'prompt_0880', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '92', '', 'Don moved to dismiss both Jacques and Jill''s claims for lack of subject matter jurisdiction. On the issue of "complete diversity," which of the following state- ments is most nearly correct?', 'The court has alienage jurisdic- tion over Jacques'' claim but must dismiss Jill''s claim because she is a citizen of California. 106 CIVIL PROCEDURE', 'The court has alienage jurisdic- tion over both Jacques and Jill''s claims because Jill takes on the domicile of her husband.', 'The court does not have diversity jurisdiction over either Jacques or Jill''s claims because Jill''s California citizenship destroys complete diversity.', 'The court has diversity juris- diction over both Jacques and Jill''s claims because Jacques is a French citizen and Jill is an Arizona citizen.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('567', 'mbe', 'mbe_0567', 'prompt_0881', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '94', '', 'Assume, for purposes of the remainder of this series of questions, that the court denied Don''s motion with respect to both Jacques and Jill''s claims. Don filed and served his answer to the complaint, which included an affirmative defense to Jacques'' claim that Jacques was con- tributorily negligent. Assume that con- tributory negligence is an absolute bar to recovery. Don moved to have Jacques and Jill''s claims tried to a judge, in- stead of a jury, citing a California state statute that requires all personal injury claims asserting punitive damages to be tried by the court rather than a jury. The statute was enacted in response to skyrocketing punitive damages jury verdicts. Plaintiffs opposed Don''s mo- tion, asserting their 7th Amendment right to a jury trial of all issues relating to their damage claims. On the "jury trial" issue, which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The "Erie" doctrine requires the court to apply the California stat- ute because that statute embodies important substantive interests.', 'The Supreme Court''s ruling in Hanna v. Plumer does not require the federal district court to apply the 7th Amendment because it is not a "Federal Rule of Civil Procedure."', 'The Supremacy Clause of the U.S. Constitution requires the federal district court to apply the 7th Amendment to Jill''s jury trial issue.', 'The federal district court can ap- ply the California statute because there will be no outcome differ- ence between a judge vs. a jury trial.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('568', 'mbe', 'mbe_0568', 'prompt_0882', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '95', '', 'For purposes of the remainder of this series of questions, assume that the court denied Don''s motion in Ques- tion 94. After a jury trial, during which Jacques offered minimal evidence of damages on his consortium claim and Don offered substantial evidence that plaintiffs'' marriage was in trouble, the jury rendered the following verdict: (1) On Jacques'' claim, in favor of Don. (2) On Jill''s claim, in favor of Jill for $80,000. Judgment was entered upon the verdict on April 1, 1993. Jacques filed a California state court suit against Don for his personal injuries caused by the collision with Don''s car. Don''s answer included an affirmative defense that Jacques was contributorily negli- gent. Don moved to dismiss Jacques'' claim based upon the April 1, 1993 judgment in Jacques and Jill v. Don. As- sume the California law of res judicata defines "cause of action" according to the "primary rights" test. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'The court should deny Don''s motion because, applying the primary rights test, Jacques did not split his cause of action.', 'The court should grant Don''s motion because, applying the "same transaction test," Jacques split his cause of action. 108 CIVIL PROCEDURE', 'The court should deny Don''s motion because Jacques'' loss of consortium claim in the prior (federal) suit against Don de- rives from Jill''s injury whereas Jacques'' claim in the current (state) suit asserts Jacques'' own injuries.', 'The court should grant Don''s motion because Jacques was in privity with Jill in the prior fed- eral court suit.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('569', 'mbe', 'mbe_0569', 'prompt_0883', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '97', '', 'DeWinter moved for summary judg- ment, based upon the September 1, 1992 judgment of acquittal in the California murder case that had been brought against him. In this motion, DeWinter asked to court to find that the judgment of acquittal conclusively establishes, for purposes of his civil suit against Floyd''s, that DeWinter did not murder his wife. Which of the following statements is not correct?', 'Mutuality of estoppel, assuming that it was the applicable law, would not permit DeWinter to use collateral estoppel against Floyd''s, a stranger to the prior criminal proceeding.', 'The Due Process clause prevents DeWinter from using collat- eral estoppel against Floyd''s, a stranger to the prior criminal proceeding.', 'DeWinter cannot use collateral estoppel because the burdens of persuasion in the prior criminal suit and in DeWinter''s civil suit are different.', 'In order to acquit DeWinter in the criminal trial, the jury must have actually and necessarily determined that there was a rea- sonable doubt as to whether he murdered Rebecca. 110 CIVIL PROCEDURE 0 0 0 - m < CI r- x3', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('57', 'mbe', 'mbe_0057', 'prompt_0340', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '14', '', 'When will Keith have a cause of action against Sam?', 'Immediately.', 'September 10th.', 'On September 1 only.', 'September 15, or after.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('570', 'mbe', 'mbe_0570', 'prompt_0884', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '99', '', 'Assume, for the remainder of this se- ries of questions, that the court denied DeWinter''s motion in Question 98. The jury rendered a verdict for DeWinter. Two days later, Floyd''s moved to dis- miss the suit on grounds that the court lacked subject matter jurisdiction to enter judgment on the verdict. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'Floyd''s motion should be denied because, by waiting until after the jury verdict to challenge sub- ject matter jurisdiction, Floyd''s has waived this defense.', 'Floyd''s motion should be denied because the court has alienage jurisdiction.', 'Floyd''s motion should be granted because complete diversity does not exist since both DeWinter and Floyd''s are California do- miciliaries.', 'The court should deny the mo- tion because DeWinter has not shown conclusively that Re- becca''s death was accidental. CIVIL PROCEDURE 1 1 1', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('571', 'mbe', 'mbe_0571', 'prompt_0885', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '100', '', 'Assume for this question, that the court denied Floyd''s motion in Ques- tion 99. Three weeks after entry of judgment, Floyd''s moved for a new trial on grounds of jury misconduct. Floyd''s offered in support of its mo- tion the affidavit of Juliet, a juror, who affirmed that Jack, another juror, had told the jury, during its deliberations, that he had done his own "snooping around" and had been told by certain employees at Floyd''s (who spoke with Jack, not knowing he was a juror) that Floyd''s knew that DeWinter didn''t kill Rebecca and that Rebecca was a noto- rious "klutz" who probably slipped and accidentally fell off the cliff. Which of the following statements is most nearly correct?', 'If the court believes Juliet''s af- fidavit, the court should grant Floyd''s motion because evidence of extraneous prejudicial infor- mation improperly brought to the jury''s attention is admissible in federal court to impeach the verdict.', 'Even if the court believes Juliet''s affidavit, the court should deny Floyd''s motion because there is no showing that Juror Jack''s mis- conduct prejudiced the verdict.', 'If the court believes Juliet''s af- fidavit, the court should grant Floyd''s motion because evidence of extrinsic misconduct is admis- sible to impeach the verdict. 112 CIVIL PROCEDURE CIVIL PROCEDURE ANSWERS', 'Even if the court believes Juliet''s affidavit, the court should deny Floyd''s motion because it is un- timely. END OF QUESTIONS', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('572', 'mbe', 'mbe_0572', 'prompt_0886', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '1', '', 'If Quail files the suit against the state, on what constitutional clause will he rely?', 'First Amendment Non-Establish- ment of Religion.', 'First Amendment Freedom of Speech.', 'Fourteenth Amendment Due Process.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('573', 'mbe', 'mbe_0573', 'prompt_0887', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '2', '', 'If Quail is able to file the case in federal court rather than in state court, on which constitutional clause will he rely?', 'First Amendment Freedom of Religion.', 'First Amendment Freedom of Speech.', 'Fourteenth Amendment Due Process.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('574', 'mbe', 'mbe_0574', 'prompt_0888', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '3', '', 'Will Quail''s state suit prevail?', 'Yes, because the prayer exces- sively entangles a religion with public schooling.', 'Yes, because Quail''s own reli- gion is being interfered with.', 'No, because all religions are be- ing treated equally.', 'No, because Quail''s claim is not in earnest.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('575', 'mbe', 'mbe_0575', 'prompt_0889', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '4', '', 'Assume for this question only, that it is not Riverdale high which offers the benediction, but is instead the River- dale School Board which offers such, before their weekly meetings (where no students are present). Which of the following propositions are correct? I. The First Amendment is irrelevant. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 153 Questions 5 —10 are based on the following facts: The state of Columbia has made it a misde- meanor for anyone to solicit voters within 75 feet of a polling place during election day hours. Supporters of Libertarian Party Presidential candidate, Andre Marrou, frus- trated at the lack of media coverage given their candidate and believing the public would embrace their platform — but for the governmental hassling — approach voters within 50 feet of the polls on election day. Their approach is moderate. Libertarian Don Baggle approaches people entering the polls, with the following soliloquy: "Hi. My name is Don. Would you like to know what the Libertarians stand for?" If the response is positive, Don gives a five-minute descrip- tion. If the response is negative, Don offers a flyer. II. There may be fewer First Amendment protections than in Question 1. III.The purpose of the prayer needs to be discerned.', 'I only.', 'II and III only.', 'II only.', 'III only. Undercover officer, Wil Marsh, arrests Don while he is in the process of offering a pam- phlet. Subsequent to the arrest, one ounce of marijuana is found on Don''s person.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('576', 'mbe', 'mbe_0576', 'prompt_0890', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '5', '', 'If Don challenges his misdemeanor ar- rest, for soliciting voters within 75 feet, on what constitutional clause or clauses will he rely?', 'First Amendment Freedom of Religion.', 'Fifth Amendment Due Process.', 'First Amendment Freedom of Speech.', 'None of the above. 154 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('577', 'mbe', 'mbe_0577', 'prompt_0891', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '6', '', 'If Don''s claim is heard, it will be be- cause which of the following threshold requirements have been met? I. Standing. II. Ripeness. III. Mootness. IV. Don is a U.S. Citizen.', 'II only.', 'II and IV only.', 'I, II, III and IV.', 'I and II only.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('578', 'mbe', 'mbe_0578', 'prompt_0892', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '7', '', 'Provided that Don''s claim of a constitu- tional violation is heard, which standard of review will be utilized?', 'Strict scrutiny.', 'Reasonable basis.', 'The Intermediate Standard.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('579', 'mbe', 'mbe_0579', 'prompt_0893', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '9', '', 'Assume for this question only, that although the state has had this law on the books for 50 years, they have never enforced it — although several individuals have violated it. In order to attempt to gain more political coverage for the Libertarians, Don sues the state to have the law overturned. If this suit is dismissed, what will be the likely reason?', 'Mootness.', 'Bad faith of Don.', 'Ripeness.', 'Independent state grounds.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('58', 'mbe', 'mbe_0058', 'prompt_0341', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '15', '', 'Keith''s delivery of the motorcycle on September 9th is:', 'An express condition.', 'An implied condition.', 'A constructive condition prece- dent.', 'A concurrent condition.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('580', 'mbe', 'mbe_0580', 'prompt_0894', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '10', '', 'Assume for this question only, that the Columbia law is deemed unconstitu- tional, can Don be prosecuted for the drug possession?', 'Yes, because they are entirely different laws.', 'Yes, because there was no corn- gal. pelling state interest. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 155', 'Yes, because the arrest was le-', 'No. Questions 12 —14 are based on the following facts:', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('581', 'mbe', 'mbe_0581', 'prompt_0895', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '11', '', 'With the state of present technology, the manufacture of nuclear-based en- ergy has an unfortunate side-effect — the production of unusable and toxic nuclear waste. Because of the world and nation''s dwindling supply of oil, the state of X has encouraged in-state business to embark on the production of nuclear energy. In order to give this en- couragement "teeth," a 20% discount is given to in-state corporations regarding the disposal of their nuclear waste. State X, itself, owns all of the nuclear- disposal plants. (With that policy, it ensures competent disposal). Y Corp, a corporation in State Y (a state bordering State X) is in the business of producing nuclear energy. Much of the energy it produces is sold to the citizens and businesses in State X. If Y Corp challenges State X for not giving it a 20% discount on disposal, its challenge will:', 'Prevail, because of the Com- merce Clause.', 'Prevail, because of the Due Pro- cess Clause.', 'Prevail, because of the Equal Protection Clause.', 'Fail, because of the sovereignty of state regulation. The city of Simmee is predominantly Cauca- sian, middle-class and blue-collar. Because of a recent jury trial verdict which was un- popular with minority communities, Simmee became a very popular spot for non-violent civil rights demonstrations. The Simmee Board of Directors, acting at the direction of Simmee''s Mayor Ringer passed an ordinance requiring all of those who wish to parade or demonstrate to apply for a permit and pay a fee based o', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('582', 'mbe', 'mbe_0582', 'prompt_0896', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '12', '', 'On what basis is the FFL suit most likely based?', 'Freedom of Association.', 'Freedom of Speech.', 'Equal Protection.', 'Due Process.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('583', 'mbe', 'mbe_0583', 'prompt_0897', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '13', '', 'Which of the following propositions are true? 156 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW I. The Simmee licensing Questions 15 — 16 are based on the procedure was a prior following facts: restraint. The city of Dickens, because of its many, II. The Simmee licensing naturally-occurring rivers, is always build- procedure was a reason- ing bridges. Unfortunately, the city fails to able time, place and man- either train or warn its employees about the ner restriction. dangers of this job. Bill Richards is on the city payroll and dies in a bridge-related ac- III. The city''s procedure was cident. Bill''s widow, Sharon, sues the city not "state action." based on §1983 U.S. Code.', 'I only.', 'II only.', 'III only.', 'II and III only.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('584', 'mbe', 'mbe_0584', 'prompt_0898', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '14', '', 'If the Simmee procedure is declared unconstitutional, it will be because of which of the following reasons: I. It was not narrowly tai- lored to meet the city''s interest. II. It vested too much power in the Board.', 'I and II only.', 'I only.', 'II only.', 'Neither I nor II.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('585', 'mbe', 'mbe_0585', 'prompt_0899', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '15', '', 'For a § 1983 claim to succeed, there must be:', 'A tort on the part of the city.', 'A crime on the part of the city.', 'A violation of constitutional rights by the city.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('586', 'mbe', 'mbe_0586', 'prompt_0900', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '16', '', 'If Sharon claims that Bill''s substantive due process rights were abridged, her suit will:', 'Prevail, because of the negli- gence of the city.', 'Prevail, because there was a § 1983 tort.', 'Fail, because the city was not negligent.', 'None of the above. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 157 O', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('587', 'mbe', 'mbe_0587', 'prompt_0901', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '17', '', 'The fictional state of Perotia has pro- hibited all waste-disposal businesses in the state from accepting waste products produced out of the state. There is no evidence that the refused waste is toxic. Bushco, a waste-disposal business in Perotia, challenges the Perotia regula- tion. Will Bushco prevail?', 'No, because a state can refuse to allow pollution.', 'Yes, because of the Commerce Clause.', 'No, because this does not appear to be economically-based.', 'None of the above. Questions 18 — 25 are based on the following facts: John Wayne Airport, of Orange County Cali- fornia, has just adopted a law, by the power of the Orange County Port Authority, ban- ning the solicitation of funds, the distribution of literature, or the sale of written material within the common areas of its airport. The National Rifle Association (NRA), angered by the rising tide of gun regulation has targeted the Wayne Airport as a forum to make their case that the Second Ame', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('588', 'mbe', 'mbe_0588', 'prompt_0902', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '21', '', 'Will Duke''s July 6 arrest be considered', 'Yes, because he was soliciting', 'Yes, because he was a public', 'First Amendment Freedom of funds. Association.', 'The Commerce Clause. nuisance.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('589', 'mbe', 'mbe_0589', 'prompt_0903', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '19', '', 'Will the public areas in the airport be', 'Yes, because the public is al-', 'Yes, because airports have, by common custom, become a forum for the marketplace of ideas.', 'No, because many airports are privately-owned.', 'No, because there is a difference between an airport and a public park, street or sidewalk.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('59', 'mbe', 'mbe_0059', 'prompt_0342', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '16', '', 'Merry, a merchant who deals in special industrial engines, agrees to construct a special industrial engine grinder for Happy, another merchant. The cost to construct this special grinder is $50,000. Upon completion of the grinder, it was agreed that Merry was to ship the ma- chine to Happy. However, before the engine was completely finished, the warehouse containing the engine burned to the ground, completely de- stroying the engine. The fire was not the fault of anyone. Which is the most correct statement?', 'Happy''s performance is excused.', 'Happy must pay damages.', 'Merry may collect from Happy the loss not covered by the in- surance.', 'Merry may sue for breach ofcon- tract.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('590', 'mbe', 'mbe_0590', 'prompt_0904', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '22', '', 'Will Duke''s July 8 arrest be considered valid?', 'Yes, because he was soliciting funds.', 'Yes, because cacti are potentially hazardous in air flights. 159 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'Yes, because there were other ways for Duke to have his ex- pression heard.', 'No, because it was an unreason- able restriction on his freedom of expression.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('591', 'mbe', 'mbe_0591', 'prompt_0905', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '23', '', 'Should the trial court use the compelling interest test in evaluating the Orange County ordinance?', 'I, II, III and IV.', 'I and III only.', 'I and IV only.', 'No, because the airport was truly not a public forum.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('592', 'mbe', 'mbe_0592', 'prompt_0906', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '24', '', 'Should the trial court use the "reason- ableness" test regarding the July 8 ar- rest?', 'Yes, because this was not a pub- lic forum.', 'No, because the airport was a public forum.', 'No, because there is no "reason- ableness" test.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('593', 'mbe', 'mbe_0593', 'prompt_0907', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '26', '', 'On what grounds will they most likely following facts: base this challenge? The year is 1999 and the U.S. has turned to the use of nuclear energy, due to the dwin- dling supply of its own oil, the Arab Oil Embargo of 1996, and the development of nuclear fusion energy (which produces only low level nuclear waste). However, many of the states have set up barriers to the disposal of low level nuclear waste in their state. The effect of this state action is to create a crisis situation regarding disposal of this waste. After much debate Congress has reacted to the problem by enacting the Comprehensive Waste Disposal Act of 1999. There are three clauses:', 'Commerce Clause.', 'Tenth Amendment.', 'Ninth Amendment.', 'Just Compensation.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('594', 'mbe', 'mbe_0594', 'prompt_0908', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '27', '', 'Will the North California challenge succeed?', 'Yes, as to all clauses of the Act.', 'Yes, as to clauses 1 and 2, only of the Act.', 'Yes, as to clause 3, only, of the Act.', 'No.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('595', 'mbe', 'mbe_0595', 'prompt_0909', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '28', '', 'If Norris appeals to the Island State Supreme Court, should he prevail?', 'Yes, because of the Freedom of Association.', 'Yes, because there is a "case or controversy."', 'No, because the issue is moot.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('596', 'mbe', 'mbe_0596', 'prompt_0910', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '30', '', 'Assume for this question only, that the U.S. Supreme Court has accepted re- view of Norris'' case on the question of the constitutionality of the Island ballot access rules. Will he prevail?', 'Yes, because the Au Natural following facts: Party was treated unfairly.', 'Yes, if the state can show that the rules on new political parties are rationally related to a permissible government interest.', 'No, if the court relies on inde- pendent state grounds.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('597', 'mbe', 'mbe_0597', 'prompt_0911', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '33', '', 'Will Clause 3 be upheld? CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 163', 'Yes, but only as to the "medical- emergency" provision.', 'Yes, but only as to the "informed" provision.', 'Yes, but only as to the "consent" provision.', 'Yes.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('598', 'mbe', 'mbe_0598', 'prompt_0912', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '37', '', 'SpecialtyX is a mail order business located in State A. It has targeted a mailing to all grocery stores in State', 'Yes, because there are no mini- mum contacts.', 'Yes, because there is a Com- merce Clause violation.', 'No, because there is a substantial nexus.', 'No, because there is not a sub- stantial nexus. 164 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW Questions 38 — 39 are based on the following facts: Late one evening Skin, a teenager, and two of his friends trespassed onto the front yard of Omar, a refugee from Iraq. Omar now lives in the fictional state of Clintonia. Skin built a Cross and burned it in Omar''s yard. Skin''s two friends escaped, but Skin was apprehended. As Skin was apprehended and Omar watched, Skin yelled to Omar, "I''ll kill you, you bloody Arab, if you', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('599', 'mbe', 'mbe_0599', 'prompt_0913', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '39', '', 'Will Skin''s post-arrest threat to Omar suffice to find him guilty under the Clintonia Hate Crime Law?', 'Yes, because all of the elements of the crime appear to have been met.', 'Yes, because these were fighting words.', 'No, because there was no mens rea in the crime.', 'No, because the law was facially unconstitutional. "It is a class 1 misdemeanor, to burn a cross, display a swastika or state any racial epithet for the purpose of expressing racial or ethnic hatred in a public place or on private prop- erty which is not in the lawful possession of the suspect."', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('6', 'mbe', 'mbe_0006', 'prompt_0032', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'property', '60', '', 'REAL PROPERTY Question 6 Roughacre & Ss Woodlot S&S Smoothacre Public Road The above chart indicates the configuration of three parcels of property located in State Blue, which has a 15-year prescription and adverse possession period. Seventeen years ago, Wallace purchased Woodlot, which is adjacent to Smooth- acre and Roughacre. At the time Wallace pur- chased Woodlot, he asked Arnold, the owner of Smoothacre, if Arnold would grant Wallace an easement to use the paved way across Smoothacre to haul logs to the public road. Arnold was an affable person and he readily granted the ease- ment to Wallace. Wallace paid nothing for the easement, and the deed granting the easement was properly recorded in the office of the county recorder of deeds. For six months Wallace hauled logs across Smoothacre. However, Wallace decided it would be easier to haul the logs over Roughacre, because the distance to the public road was less using that route. Wallace negotiated with Brenda, the owner of Roughacre, and Brenda granted Wallace a license to haul logs on the gravel road across Roughacre to the public road. Wallace paid Brenda a modest annual fee for this privilege. In the intervening time period, Wallace never used the paved way across Smoothacre for any purpose. Six months ago, Arnold conveyed Smoothacre to Cyd, and two weeks ago, Cyd had a chain-link fence built around the perim- eter of Smoothacre which, of course, blocked Wallace’s access to the paved way crossing Smoothacre. One week ago, acting within her rights, Brenda revoked Wallace’s license to use the gravel road crossing Roughacre. The next day, Wallace told Cyd, “Open up that chain- link fence where Smoothacre abuts Woodlot, because I have an easement to haul my logs across your paved way.” Cyd replied, “Forget it, Buster. I never agreed to any easement, and I don’t want you hauling logs across my property.” Wallace seeks advice about his rights in the easement across Smoothacre. Which of the following best represen', 'Bart wins, because he has a fee simple subject to divestment.', 'Bart loses, because Newt is Homer’s tenant and not Bart’s.', 'Newt wins, because Bart is attempting a retaliatory eviction.', 'Newt loses, because Homer’s conveyance cuts off Newt’s tenancy. GO ON TO THE NEXT PAGE', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('60', 'mbe', 'mbe_0060', 'prompt_0343', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '17', '', 'Flint Stone Co., a major constructor, agreed to build a large enclosed shop- ping mall in Bedrock, for the Rubble Corporation. After long hours of delib- eration, the parties agreed on the price of 30 million dollars. The contract was signed on January 1. The completion of the new mall was set for May 1. As Flint started digging, it struck jelly- stone. Removing this jellystone would increase the cost by one-half million dollars. Other builders who made bids had been aware ofthe jellystone and had submitted much higher bids. The most probable outcome is:', 'Flint Stone Co. will be excused from performance.', 'Flint Stone and Rubble will split the increased costs.', 'The contract is void due to im- practicability.', 'Flint Stone Co. will be bound to finish the work originally con- tracted for and will suffer the in- creased costs. CONTRACTS iii Questions 18 -19 are based on the following facts: Brent, a buyer, contracted with Selma, a seller, to buy 12 dozen roller blades for $1,200. Payment was due 20 days after de- livery. When the roller blades arrived they were found to be non-conforming. Brent re- jected the shipment of roller blades.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('600', 'mbe', 'mbe_0600', 'prompt_0914', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '38', '', 'Will Skin''s burning of the Cross on Omar''s lawn suffice to find him guilty under the Clintonia Hate Crime Law?', 'Yes, because all of the elements of the crime appear to have been met.', 'Yes, because Skin''s acts were constitutionally unprotected.', 'No, because there was no mens rea in the crime.', 'No, because the law was over- broad. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 165 Questions 40 — 42 are based on the following law: Sick and tired of the profiteering of criminal convicts and their attorneys, the state of Bronsonia has enacted the following law, §208:', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('601', 'mbe', 'mbe_0601', 'prompt_0915', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '40', '', 'Famed football star, Mike Toggle, was convicted of rape in Bronsonia. In order to set the record straight, he contracted with Violence Press to write a book. In the book, Toggle denied having raped the victim, although he did admit to rap- ing a female when he was 16-years-old. Toggle brought up this point to indicate how guilty he felt at that time and, thus, why he would never commit this crime. Violence Press paid Toggle an advance of $5,000 and, to date, $2,333 in royalties. Which of the following propositions are true? I. Violence Press violated §208.2. II. Toggle violated §208.1.', 'I only.', 'II only.', 'I and II.', 'Neither I nor II.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('602', 'mbe', 'mbe_0602', 'prompt_0916', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '41', '', 'Rotty has been convicted of several "contract killings." Famed writer Jack Junkins decides to write his story. He interviews Rotty over a period of two months. If Bronsonia prosecutes Junk- ins under §208, they will:', 'Succeed, because he profited from crime.', 'Succeed, because he wrote about crime. 166 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'Succeed, because he profited from another''s crime.', 'Fail, because Junkins was only morally guilty.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('603', 'mbe', 'mbe_0603', 'prompt_0917', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '42', '', 'Which of the following propositions are true? I. The Bronsonia statute is unconstitutional since it is not narrowly tailored to further a compelling state interest. II. The Bronsonia statute is unconstitutional since it is overbroad. III. The Bronsonia statute is a kind of prior restraint on the press and speech.', 'I, II and III only.', 'I and II only.', 'I and III only.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('604', 'mbe', 'mbe_0604', 'prompt_0918', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '43', '', 'Due to migration to the Southern and Western states, the fictional state of Northwest has experienced a marked decrease in population — particularly as compared to the growth of population in the Southern and Western states. In order to facilitate proportional represen- tation in the U.S. House of Representa- tives, Congress has increased the seats available in these states, and decreased the seats in Northwest, but not beyond that accounted for by increased or de- creased population. If Northwest chal- lenges this reduction, it will:', 'Succeed, since states must be given equal representation.', 'Fail, because states must be given equal representation.', 'Fail, because the facts seem to indicate an equal apportionment method was used.', 'None of the above. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 167 The year is 1993. As of 1954, in the land- mark decision of Brown v. Board of Edu- cation, 347 U.S. 483, the fictional state of Elizabethia has had its primary, secondary and post-secondary schools under the super- vision of the local Federal District Court. The supervision was based on Elizabethia''s former pattern of de jure segregation. In that era, Elizabethia assigned students to school solely depending on the race of the student. As of 1993, all enr', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('605', 'mbe', 'mbe_0605', 'prompt_0919', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '45', '', 'If Elizabethia''s school administrators are presently found to be in good faith, are the U.S. Supreme Court and its lower federal courts discharged from supervising desegregation?', 'Yes, provided admission policies are race-neutral.', 'Yes, because there is no more that can be accomplished.', 'I and IV only. following facts: 168 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW Questions 46 — 48 are based on the following facts: The city of Marblestone, in the fictional state of Alfredia, has enacted a rent-control ordi- nance. The ordinance is specifically aimed at controlling the rent in mobile home parks. The policy of the rent control ordinance is to protect the lower-middle classes and elderly — who are the most frequent tenants of the parks. The ordinance at issue not only "freezes" the rent for those now ten', 'No. Questions 44 — 45 are based on the', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('606', 'mbe', 'mbe_0606', 'prompt_0920', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '46', '', 'Daniel files suit in federal district court, to overturn the ordinance. Daniel files this suit because he is against govern- mental regulation of the private sector. If Daniel''s suit is not heard, it will be because of which of the following rea- sons: I. He lacks standing. II. The case is not ripe. III.There is no case or con- troversy. IV.There is no federal ques- tion.', 'I and II only.', 'I, II and III only.', 'I, II, III and IV.', 'I, II and IV only.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('607', 'mbe', 'mbe_0607', 'prompt_0921', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '47', '', 'If Norris files a federal suit, because he had intended to buy a mobile home park, and now wonders about the wis- dom of his potential investment, will his case be heard?', 'No, because the case is not ripe.', 'Yes, because there was a govern- mental taking.', 'Yes, because there was a de- nial of the Just Compensation Clause.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('608', 'mbe', 'mbe_0608', 'prompt_0922', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '48', '', 'If the owner of a Marblestone mobile home park, who can substantiate that she has been prevented from raising rent in her park, files a federal suit to overturn the ordinance based on an abrogation of the Just Compensation Clause, this suit will:', 'Yes, since both parties consent- ed.', 'Yes, since trials never need be public.', 'Yes, since divorce trials can be closed.', 'Succeed. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 169 Questions 49 — 53 are based on the following facts: Famous movie director Billy Eucalyptus and his wife Marci Sparrow are about to enter trial regarding their contested divorce. judge so close the trial?', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('609', 'mbe', 'mbe_0609', 'prompt_0923', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '50', '', 'If only Billy consents, can the judge preclude the press and public from the trial?', 'No, since both parties must con- sent.', 'Yes, since trials never need be public.', 'Yes, since divorce trials can be closed.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('61', 'mbe', 'mbe_0061', 'prompt_0344', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '18', '', 'Brent still has the roller blades and is required to:', 'Send the roller blades back to Selma.', 'Sell the roller blades at a discount and give the proceeds to Selma.', 'Accept the roller blades and sue for breach of contract.', 'Hold the roller blades with rea- sonable care for disposition as Selma instructs.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('610', 'mbe', 'mbe_0610', 'prompt_0924', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '52', '', 'Assume for this question only, that after the dissolution of marriage proceed- ing, Billy is formally charged with the sexual assault of Marci''s 15-year-old daughter. Billy moves to keep the press out of the trial — based on his claim that the minor daughter''s life will be ruined. Will Billy''s request be granted?', 'Yes, if a transcript of the trial is made public soon after the trial.', 'Yes, if the minor consents.', 'Yes, since it is a minor who is involved.', 'No. 170 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('611', 'mbe', 'mbe_0611', 'prompt_0925', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '53', '', 'Assume for this question only, that Billy moves that the dissolution of marriage trial be open to television transmission. The court''s denial of this motion:', 'Violates the First Amendment.', 'Violates the Sixth Amendment.', 'May be a permissible state rule.', 'None of the above. Questions 54 — 58 are based on the following facts: Jessica Rabindra purports to follow an an- cient Delphic Religion, which she, herself, in psychic trance, has rediscovered. The religion follows the so-called "goddess" perception of cosmic reality. She has formed a church called, "The Church of the Astral Goddess." The Church is located on a one- acre secluded ranch in the fictional state of North Californ. Membership in the Church is voluntary. But each member, to be admitt', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('612', 'mbe', 'mbe_0612', 'prompt_0926', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '54', '', 'If Jessica is charged under § 1111, and offers a First Amendment Freedom of Religion defense, she will most likely:', 'Succeed, because she was exer- cising a religion.', 'Fail, because the Freedom of Religion does not protect against prosecution for prostitution.', 'Succeed, because the adults con- sented.', 'Fail, because her religion was bogus.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('613', 'mbe', 'mbe_0613', 'prompt_0927', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '55', '', 'Jessica''s best argument against a pros- ecution under § 1112 is:', 'There were other activities of the church besides sex and the pay- ment of money was not required to have sex with Jessica.', 'She was the only priestess.', 'Other activities occurred in her temple.', 'She was exercising the Freedom of Religion.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('614', 'mbe', 'mbe_0614', 'prompt_0928', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '56', '', 'If Jessica is prosecuted for a violation of § 1114, her prosecution will likely:', 'Succeed, because there is no right to extra-marital sexual pri- vacy.', 'Fail, because the acts were pri- vate and consensual.', 'Fail, because this crime does not include sex-for-hire.', 'Fail, because there was no genital penetration.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('615', 'mbe', 'mbe_0615', 'prompt_0929', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '57', '', 'If Jessica is prosecuted for a violation of §2009, based on the inducement to join her church in her book, her best defense will be:', 'Freedom of Religion.', 'Freedom of Speech.', 'The penumbra right to privacy.', 'Freedom of Association. 172 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('616', 'mbe', 'mbe_0616', 'prompt_0930', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '59', '', 'If Rex Bookstore challenges this law,', 'Succeed, because of the Freedom of the Press.', 'Succeed, because of the Com- merce Clause.', 'Succeed, because of the Estab- lishment Clause.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('617', 'mbe', 'mbe_0617', 'prompt_0931', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '60', '', 'If Ralph''s Kosher Minimarket chal- lenges this law on First Amendment Establishment Clause grounds, they will:', 'Succeed, because the law is for the purpose of enforcing the Christian Sabbath.', 'Succeed, because it impedes their Free Exercise of Religion.', 'Succeed, because it is a taking, without Just Compensation.', 'None of the above. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 173 Q Questions 61— 62 are based on the following facts: The fictional state of Rightwing has changed the motto on their license plates to "JUST SAY NO." Ted Paine is offended by this since he believes it is contrary to the prin- ciples of his Asian religion to demand others behave in a certain way. So, Ted alters his plate to read "JUST SAY NOT." The Righ- twing State Police arrested Ted for defacing public property (since the plates technically belong to t', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('618', 'mbe', 'mbe_0618', 'prompt_0932', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '61', '', 'What is the likely ground of Ted''s ap- peal suggested by the closest Supreme Court precedent?', 'First Amendment Free Speech.', 'First Amendment Freedom of Religion.', 'Fourth Amendment Search and Seizure.', 'First Amendment Freedom of Association.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('619', 'mbe', 'mbe_0619', 'prompt_0933', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '63', '', 'Grade school student, Jeremiah, refuses to salute the flag in his second grade class. He is disciplined by his teacher, Ms. Thomas; and sent to the principal, Dr. Rehnquist, to serve 2 hours of deten- tion. He had refused to salute the flag based on his upbringing as a Jehovah''s Witness, that one''s allegiance is owed only to God. His mother, who happens to be a lawyer, sues the school board, seeking an injunction of forced flag- saluting and also seeking unspecified damages. Will Jeremiah''s suit pre- vail?', 'No, because this was not truly prejudicial to his religion.', 'No, because the Constitution protects family values, such as saluting the flag.', 'No, because he could still as- sociate with members of his religion.', 'None of the above. 174 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW Questions 64 — 65 are based on the following facts: The agrarian, back-to-nature sect called the Followers of Landrew, believe that a child, as of age 14, should not go to public school and should begin to work the fields as part of their farm life. Archon, the parent of Jedidiah (who is 14) takes him out of the local public junior high school. However, the school district has a rule that children must attend school until their sixteenth birthday.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('62', 'mbe', 'mbe_0062', 'prompt_0345', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '19', '', 'In a general claim for damages, which would not be incidental damages?', 'An expected profit by the buyer of which the seller was aware at the time the contract was made.', 'Expenses incurred in inspecting non-conforming goods.', 'The cost of storing non-conform- ing goods. m CONTRACTS', 'A sales commission paid to ac- complish cover.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('620', 'mbe', 'mbe_0620', 'prompt_0934', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '64', '', 'Archon is ordered to re-enroll Jedidiah in school, and is threatened with con- tempt of court if he does not. If Archon challenges this order, he will:', 'I and II only.', 'II and III only.', 'Fail, because there is a compel- ling interest in the education of minors.', 'Succeed, because the school district lacks jurisdiction.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('621', 'mbe', 'mbe_0621', 'prompt_0935', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '66', '', 'The religion of Funkamental, now established for 50 years, has fallen on hard times. In the eyes of Ron, Hub, the present president, has allowed the assets and membership of the religion to drastically fall. But Hub will not give up his presidency. So, Ron files suit in the state court to order Hub to stand for a democrat election. Will Ron prevail?', 'No, because of the Free Exercise Clause.', 'No, because of the Establishment Clause. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 175', 'No, because there is no case or Questions 67 — 71 are based on the controversy. following facts:', 'Yes, if the facts establish Ron''s claim to be true. The city of Puritania in the fictional state of Hypocritia, troubled by the decay in the moral fabric of its society, has enacted a statute barring nudity in places open to the public. The statute punishes such as a misde- meanor. Nudity is defined as the "exposure of female or male genitalia or the exposure of female nipples or areola, when such is done for the purpose of appealing to the prurient interest."', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('622', 'mbe', 'mbe_0622', 'prompt_0936', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '67', '', 'On its face, is the Puritania statute un- constitutional?', 'Yes, because there can be sym- bolic speech aspects to both male and female nudity.', 'Yes, because it is overbroad.', 'Yes, because it is vague.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('623', 'mbe', 'mbe_0623', 'prompt_0937', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '68', '', 'The "Go For It" Bar, open only to those over the age of 21, has a stage where women dance topless. One evening the owner, Paddy, and the offending fe- males are arrested by the police. If they appeal their conviction, they will:', 'Fail, because the Puritania law is overbroad.', 'Succeed, because the Puritania law is overbroad. 176 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'Fail, because their conduct is constitutionally unprotected.', 'Succeed, because the conduct was symbolic speech.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('624', 'mbe', 'mbe_0624', 'prompt_0938', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '69', '', 'One afternoon, Murphy Moe is arrested for breast-feeding her infant child in a frequented public park. Murphy hap- pens to be a very beautiful woman. Her act of breast-feeding included exposure of her areola and one breast or the other (while switching her baby from breast-to-breast). Murphy was wearing a button down shear blouse, and tightly- fitting, high-cut shorts. A group of men would frequently, in a clandestine way, watch Murphy each afternoon during their lunch break. After her arrest, and after being given proper Miranda warnings, Murphy ad- mits that she knew several men were watching her. She also admits that she knew her actions were likely to sexually arouse some of the men — that in fact, she wanted, "to show men the beauties of having babies and because I was hoping to meet a new man." If Murphy is prosecuted under the Puritania law, she will likely:', 'Not be convicted, because the elements of the crime have not been met.', 'Be convicted, even though she might claim her act was sym- bolic speech.', 'Be convicted, because her acts were in public.', 'Not be convicted, her conduct was not lewd and lascivious.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('625', 'mbe', 'mbe_0625', 'prompt_0939', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '70', '', 'Chipmunks, a club catering to adult fe- males, offers erotic dancing by men. In the dances, the men do a "strip tease", in which they remove all of their clothing but for a tight bikini-type bottom. The brief is so tight that outlines of the male genitalia are visible. In fact, many of the men who work at Chipmunks arrange "dates" with the customers after hours, during which they perform sex for hire contrary to another prostitution law of Puritania. The police are aware of the prostitu- tion, but are unable to find any females who are willing to testify against the men. Thus, Chipmunks, and several of the male dancers, are prosecuted under the Puritania indecent exposure statute, above. Will the prosecution be successful?', 'Yes, because they were guilty of prostitution.', 'Yes, because Chipmunks was clearly contrary to Puritania''s community standards.', 'No, because the prostitution could not be proven. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 177', 'No, because there was no genital "exposure" as under the relevant Puritania statute.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('626', 'mbe', 'mbe_0626', 'prompt_0940', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '71', '', 'Darby is a young woman out of work in Puritania. She was an aerobics instruc- tor at one of the city''s health clubs; but due to the recession the club has closed down. So, Darby takes out a vendor''s license to sell hot dogs in one of Puritania''s open air malls. Unlike the other vendors, however, Darby wears a thong bikini. She is arrested for violat- ing the Puritania statute. Will Darby''s First Amendment "symbolic speech" argument succeed?', 'Yes, because she was doing noth- ing unlawful.', 'Yes, because her sales approach had artistic First Amendment content.', 'No, because there was no speech content to her acts or to the city''s prosecution of her.', 'No, because she was guilty of the crime.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('627', 'mbe', 'mbe_0627', 'prompt_0941', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '72', '', 'The fictional state of South Kaliforn has pictured on its flag a western gunslinger. Because Duke finds not only guns, but the whole gunslinger mentality patently offensive, he calls a press conference and publicly burns the South Kaliforn flag. In South Kaliforn, it is a misde- meanor to: "Purposely burn, destroy, mutilate, defile, desecrate or dirty the state or federal flag." Duke is pros- ecuted and convicted in South Kaliforn. Will his federal appeal succeed?', 'Yes, because the crime was strict liability and, thus, overbroad.', 'Yes, because the state infringed his First Amendment rights.', 'No, because the flag he burned was a state flag.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('628', 'mbe', 'mbe_0628', 'prompt_0942', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '73', '', 'The year is 2017, and you may assume U.S. Constitutional law has remained the same as it is at present — with one notable exception. The U.S. Constitu- tion has been amended to place an outright and complete ban on the use of alcohol, with the only exception being for prescription drugs. Father 0'' Scalia, a Catholic Priest living in Illinois, is prosecuted for using wine during the Sacrament at his Church. If he challenges the law based on First Amendment grounds, he will: 178 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW Questions 74 — 75 are based on the following facts: Sick and tired of the poor quality of ap- pointees to the U.S. Supreme Court, both houses of Congress pass a law whereby the power to appoint U.S. Supreme Court judges is stripped from the President and resides solely in the houses. The so-called "Supreme Judiciary Act" is vetoed by the President; but the veto is overturned.', 'Succeed, because of the Free Exercise Clause.', 'Succeed, because there is no compelling state interest.', 'Succeed, because of the Freedom of Association Clause.', 'Fail.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('629', 'mbe', 'mbe_0629', 'prompt_0943', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '74', '', 'What majority of Congress is necessary to overturn a presidential veto?', 'Two-thirds of either house.', 'Two-thirds of the Senate.', 'Two-thirds of the House of Rep- resentatives.', 'Two-thirds of both houses.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('63', 'mbe', 'mbe_0063', 'prompt_0346', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '20', '', 'Tess sees a demonstration of a new model curling iron that curls hair in 30 seconds. Tess is interested because she is a model and always has to do her hair in a hurry. The curling iron that Tess purchases takes 2 minutes to curl her hair. Is the curling iron''s performance sufficient to create a cause of action for Tess?', 'No, because she has no privity.', 'Yes, because there was a war- ranty of fitness for a particular purpose.', 'Yes, because there was an ex- press warranty.', 'None of the above. Questions 21 - 25 are based on the following facts: Girkin, the local City Councilman, owns a fast-food restaurant at 1st and Meridian. Because ofhis special position, he learns the Mayor and the Governor are making the fi- nal arrangements to move the state hockey team, the "Knuckleheads," to City. The ten-acre proposed site is very close to 1st and Meridian. Various general contractors are submitting bids to construct the sports complex. Girkin spends a large amount of money', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('630', 'mbe', 'mbe_0630', 'prompt_0944', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '75', '', 'Assume for this question only, that the President challenges Congress'' power to enact this act. Which of the following propositions are correct? I. Federal judicial selection is solely a presidential decision. II. The Act is unconstitu- tional.', 'I and II.', 'I only. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 179', 'II only. Questions 76 — 77 are based on the following facts:', 'None of the above. During a senate debate, U.S. Senator Tom Semand argues that the scientific work pro- duced by XYZ University "is not up to the standards of grammar school — let alone fit for a $150,000 federal grant".', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('631', 'mbe', 'mbe_0631', 'prompt_0945', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '76', '', 'If XYZ University sues Semand for defamation, his best defense will be:', 'First Amendment Freedom of Speech.', 'Article I Speech and Debate Clause.', 'XYZ was a public entity.', 'Sovereign immunity.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('632', 'mbe', 'mbe_0632', 'prompt_0946', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '77', '', 'Assume for this question only, that it can be proven that Semand received a $20,000 bribe from ABC University, the competitor of XYZ University, for the $150,000 grant. May Semand be sued for taking a bribe in connection with the federal grant application?', 'No, since there was sovereign immunity.', 'No, because this kind of thing is difficult to prove.', 'No, because of the Speech and Debate Clause.', 'None of the above. 180 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 0 = 0 Questions 78 — 79 are based on the following facts: Governor Pilson, governor of the fictional state of Columbia, is indicted in Columbia for the embezzlement of state funds. United States President George, convinced that such is a paltry political attempt to destroy one of his closest allies, pardons Pilson for "any and all crimes committed while in office, up to and including those committed as of this date."', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('633', 'mbe', 'mbe_0633', 'prompt_0947', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '78', '', 'If the Columbia Attorney General chal- lenges this pardon, she will:', 'Fail, because such is one of the enumerated presidential pow- ers.', 'Fail, because the President''s power is limited to pardon for political crimes.', 'Succeed, because the Presi- dent''s power is limited to federal crimes.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('634', 'mbe', 'mbe_0634', 'prompt_0948', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '80', '', 'The ambassador to the United States from the fictional foreign republic of Sheisterdam, while touring the United States, gets drunk while driving in the state of Oregon and is arrested by the Oregon police. In fact, the ambassador was not legally drunk and was unaware of the Oregon prohibition of driving while intoxicated. The local district attorney decides to prosecute. What is the ambassador''s best defense?', 'Yes, if the crimes were commit- ted while Pilson was in office. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 181', 'He was not drunk.', 'He was ignorant of the law.', 'Oregon does not have jurisdic- tion over foreigners in their state.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('635', 'mbe', 'mbe_0635', 'prompt_0949', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '83', '', 'What is the state''s best argument why Eleventh Amendment. their prohibition should be upheld?', 'There is no constitutional right Tenth Amendment. to engage in consensual homo- sexuality.', 'There is no constitutional right', 'Be dismissed, because of the', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('636', 'mbe', 'mbe_0636', 'prompt_0950', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '84', '', 'What is the defendants'' best argu- ment?', 'There is a constitutional right to', 'There was a Due Process Clause violation.', 'The state rule served no rational basis. 182 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'The state had no compelling interest.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('637', 'mbe', 'mbe_0637', 'prompt_0951', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '85', '', 'Under existing U.S. Supreme Court precedent, who is likely to succeed on federal appeal?', 'I and II only.', 'I only.', 'III only.', 'None of the above, because this is an open issue.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('638', 'mbe', 'mbe_0638', 'prompt_0952', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '87', '', 'Seeme Clothiers, of Lemon County, challenges the outright ban. Will they succeed?', 'No, because such is part of the state''s police powers.', 'No, because of the Privileges and Immunities Clause.', 'No, because the President is the Commander-in-Chief.', 'Yes, because of the Tenth Amend- ment.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('639', 'mbe', 'mbe_0639', 'prompt_0953', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '89', '', 'Assume for this question only, that Lemon County has also banned all legal and medical advertising — because they are "unprofessional and have decreased the quality of services." How should these regulations be analyzed if chal- lenged? Questions 87 — 89 are based on the', 'Vagueness Doctrine.', 'Ripeness Doctrine.', 'Commercial Speech Doctrine.', 'Just Compensation Clause.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('64', 'mbe', 'mbe_0064', 'prompt_0347', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '21', '', 'If SK Enterprises fails to perform, can Mayor sue GK, Inc.?', 'No, because Mayor had no do- native intent towards the SK En- terprises.', 'No, because Mayor would be a creditor beneficiary if she had advanced salary to GK, Inc.', 'Yes, because Mayor would be a creditor beneficiary.', 'Yes, because Mayor would have vested rights in the contract be- tween SK Enterprises and GK, Inc.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('640', 'mbe', 'mbe_0640', 'prompt_0954', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '90', '', 'The fictional state of Barrier requires all of its attorneys to pass a moral fit- ness exam in order to be licensed. The burden is on the applicant to show good moral character. Applicant Spendthrift, 10 years earlier, had declared bank- ruptcy. In the last 8 years, however, he has established a fine credit rating and has held a steady job. Spendthrift, after passing the bar, is denied a law license by the Barrier Supreme Court, due to his prior bankruptcy. What will be Spendthrift''s best argument on federal appeal?', 'He is now morally fit.', 'Significant time has passed.', 'Barrier''s ruling is contrary to Congress''s intent to allow bank- rupt persons to start over.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('641', 'mbe', 'mbe_0641', 'prompt_0955', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '92', '', 'If Junior is summarily suspended for one month from Jalonia Junior High School, his constitutional rights have been: II. Yes, if he was punished because it was burned in an unreasonable time, place and manner. III.No, because there was no hearing. IV. No, because there is a right to burn the U.S. flag in political protest.', 'Denied, because he was not given a prior hearing.', 'Denied, because flag-burning is a right.', 'Not denied, because it was not a specific political protest.', 'Not denied, because it was like shouting "Fire!" in a crowded theatre.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('642', 'mbe', 'mbe_0642', 'prompt_0956', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '94', '', 'Assume for this question only, that Junior''s parents take him out of the Jalonia Junior High School, and enroll him in the Bush Academy, a private school. Junior again burns a U.S. flag. What is Junior''s best argument in ap- pealing this suspension?', 'Procedural due process.', 'Substantive due process.', 'First Amendment.', 'None of the above. I. Yes, if he was punished because it was not his flag. 186 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('643', 'mbe', 'mbe_0643', 'prompt_0957', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '95', '', 'Intent on restoring family values, the state of Alabama has just enacted a statute whereby illegitimate children cannot sue in tort for the wrongful death of their parents. Is this a lawful state regulation?', 'Yes, because of the Tenth Amend- ment.', 'Yes, because Alabama has a permissible interest in so legis- lating.', 'Yes, because Alabama has a ra- tional basis in so legislating.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('644', 'mbe', 'mbe_0644', 'prompt_0958', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '97', '', 'The state of Hawaii legislature, still angered at the gross inequities placed on the many by the few old landown- ers before the state made that conduct illegal, pronounces a list of the twenty worst offenders. It also fines them $25,000 each and declares them guilty of a "misdemeanor against the People of the State of Hawaii." They are not sentenced, however, to any time in jail. Under which of the following consti- tutional clauses will these individuals have a successful claim? I. Just Compensation. II. Fifth Amendment Due Process. III.The Bills of Attainder Clause. IV.The Ex Post Facto Clause.', 'I, II, III and IV.', 'III and IV only.', 'II only.', 'None of the above, since no jail time was imposed. CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 187', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('645', 'mbe', 'mbe_0645', 'prompt_0959', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '98', '', 'Fictional President George, after a highly difficult, but successful, re-elec- tion campaign, feeling duly grateful to his Chief of Staff Snaker, bestows upon him the title of "Royal Knight." The title has no significance whatsoever, but there is an official ceremony at the White House. Ned Rader challenges this gesture in federal court. Will Rader succeed?', 'No, because the title has no sig- nificance.', 'Yes, because it is a title of Nobil- ity.', 'No, because it is analogous to a Presidential Medal.', 'No, because Rader has no stand- ing. Questions 99 — 100 are based on the following facts: Rawson is sentenced to death at the capital- sentencing phase of his murder prosecution. Over objection of the defense, the jury is told that Rawson is a member of a white supremacist group. Rawson is sentenced to death and appeals the conviction based on the prosecutor''s allusion to his membership in the white supremacist group.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('646', 'mbe', 'mbe_0646', 'prompt_0960', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '99', '', 'What will Rawson''s best constitutional argument be?', 'That he is being subjected to Cruel and Unusual Punishment.', 'That his Freedom of Association Rights were abridged.', 'That his Free Speech rights were abridged.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('647', 'mbe', 'mbe_0647', 'prompt_0961', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '100', '', 'If Rawson''s white supremacist group did not advocate disobedience to the law, and if both Rawson and the victim are white, will Rawson prevail?', 'No, because Victim Impact state- ments are admissible.', 'No, because the error was harm- less.', 'No, because it was relevant.', 'Yes, because it was irrelevant. 188 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('648', 'mbe', 'mbe_0648', 'prompt_0962', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '101', '', 'A story is written about movie star Birdie Jones, in the tabloid, the Nation- al Perspirer. In the story, it is revealed that Birdie is infected with genital herpes. If Jones sues the newspaper in tort for defamation, she will:', 'Prevail, because such is damag- ing.', 'Prevail, if the story was know- ingly false.', 'Prevail, since she has a right to privacy.', 'Fail, because she does not have a right to privacy. ND OF QUESTIONS CONSTITUTIONAL LAW 189 190 CONSTITUTIONAL LAW CONSTITUTIONAL LAW ANSWERS A is the correct answer. Junior''s claim is one of the Establishment of Religion in violation of First Amendment. B is in- correct because it is not a Speech claim. It is true that First Amendment claims against a state entity are made applica- ble against the state by the incorporation of some of the Bill of Rights under the Fourteenth Amendment. However, t', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('649', 'mbe', 'mbe_0649', 'prompt_0963', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '1', '', 'Mick is on a rough corner waiting for a bus. Officer Stone sees Mick and since he has nothing else to do, decides to investigate Mick. Stone walks up to Mick and says: "Hold it right there, mister." Mick takes flight and throws away a marijuana joint which was on his person. Mick is prosecuted for illegal marijuana possession. Mick''s attorney files a motion to suppress the marijuana. This motion will: I. Fail, because Mick has no standing. II. Fail, because Mick aban- doned the property. III. Fail, because Mick was not "seized."', 'I only.', 'II and III only.', 'I, II and III.', 'None of the above. Questions 2 — 3 are based on the following facts: Marie is arrested on a charge of solicita- tion to commit prostitution. Her arrest is performed by an undercover officer. She is brought to jail. Three days later, still in jail, she appears before a judge who refuses to let her free on bail. Marie files a writ to the state supreme court, demanding release.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('65', 'mbe', 'mbe_0065', 'prompt_0348', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '22', '', 'If Mayor sues GK, Inc., can GK, Inc. sue SK Enterprises?', 'No, Mayor cannot sue GK, Inc., as the general contractor is not responsible for the breach of sub- contractors.', 'Yes, since SK Enterprises, as the subcontractor, is the obligor.', 'Yes, if Mayor and GK, Inc. have a valid enforceable contract.', 'Yes, if GK, Inc. and SK Enter- prises had a valid enforceable contract.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('650', 'mbe', 'mbe_0650', 'prompt_0964', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '3', '', 'Assuming that the state supreme court orders Marie be set free, she may have a civil claim against the arresting of- ficer.', 'True, provided he was in bad faith.', 'True, regardless of his bad faith.', 'False.', 'None of the above. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 233 Q', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('651', 'mbe', 'mbe_0651', 'prompt_0965', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '4', '', 'Since illegal drugs are often transported by transients taking Blackhound buses, Officer Grey decides to investigate the Blackhound station in his precinct. He obtains consent of the local bus station manager to enter buses and request if luggage can be searched. Grey enters a bus at 11:00 p.m., before it is to leave, then announces the following to the seven people in the bus: "Hi, my name is Jack Grey. May I search each of your bags?" One by one, Grey walks to each passenger seat and asks for their bag. Passenger Dawn hands over her purse, which has cocaine in it. At trial for Dawn''s cocaine possession, is the cocaine admissible against Dawn?', 'No, because there was no warrant to search.', 'No, because there was no exi- gency.', 'Yes, because of the automobile exception.', 'Yes, because of the consent ex- ception. Questions 5 — 7 are based on the following facts: Acid is driving his car at 70 m.p.h. in a 55 m.p.h. zone. He is pulled over by Officer Quist. Quist asks Acid if he can search the passenger compartment of Acid''s car. Acid says, "Yes." Quist finds a closed bag in the back seat and in it is LSD (contraband). Acid says, "I didn''t mean to allow you to get into that!" Quist then opens Acid''s trunk and finds a closed suitcase. Quist opens it and finds $10,000', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('652', 'mbe', 'mbe_0652', 'prompt_0966', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '5', '', 'When Quist pulled Acid over, there was a:', 'Detention.', 'De facto arrest.', 'Illegal detention.', 'Legal traffic stop.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('653', 'mbe', 'mbe_0653', 'prompt_0967', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '6', '', 'Quist''s opening of the closed bag in the passenger compartment was:', 'Beyond the scope of the consent given.', 'Within the scope of the consent given.', 'A lawful search incident to ar- rest.', 'A lawful search under the drug exception to the warrant require- ment. 234 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q - 0 `1 73Om PIE oz c > 73 mi-', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('654', 'mbe', 'mbe_0654', 'prompt_0968', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '8', '', 'Nikki is charged with forgery and requests a lawyer to represent her at trial. After the formal charging, she is released on bail. Officer Bix calls Nikki on the phone and asks her about an unrelated theft. Nikki admits to the theft. The admission is:', 'Prevail, because Rex was inef- fective.', 'Fail, because there was no preju- dice.', 'Fail, because Rex was privately retained.', 'Fail, because collateral appeal is not allowed.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('655', 'mbe', 'mbe_0655', 'prompt_0969', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '10', '', 'Prosecutor Chill, during the capital sen- tencing phase of Pip''s trial, introduces evidence of the effect of Pip''s killing of Victim Vinny on Vinny''s children. Such statement:', 'Violates due process.', 'Her reasonable expectation of privacy was abrogated. Dixona is in jail serving three years for grand theft. The jail is over-crowded and as a result, Dixona is occasionally sexually molested by other female prisoners. Dixona files a writ of habeas corpus arguing cruel and unusual punishment.', 'Her legitimate expectation of privacy was invaded.', 'She was subjected to cruel and unusual punishment.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('656', 'mbe', 'mbe_0656', 'prompt_0970', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '11', '', 'In order for the writ to succeed, the fol- lowing must be proven: I. The sexual molestation and over-crowding was "cruel." II. The sexual molestation and overcrowding was "unusual." III. The warden was "delib- erately indifferent" to Dixona''s conditions.', 'I and II only.', 'III only.', 'I, II and III.', 'None of the above.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('657', 'mbe', 'mbe_0657', 'prompt_0971', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '13', '', 'Assume for this question only, that dur- ing a strip and body-cavity search by a male guard, illegal contraband cocaine is discovered. Dixona is prosecuted for such. Her worst argument is:', 'Her Fourth Amendment rights were violated.', 'Her Due Process rights were violated.', 'Her Equal Protection rights were violated.', 'Her Eighth Amendment rights were violated.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('658', 'mbe', 'mbe_0658', 'prompt_0972', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '12', '', 'Assume for this question only, that Dixona is routinely stripped and body- cavity searched by a male guard. Her best argument via writ of habeas corpus is:', 'The trial was a farce and mock- following facts: ery. Wilber Mercer is out of work and decides', 'The judge was biased. to make a "few bucks, quick." In order to do such, he arranges to sell cocaine. He is', 'Counsel was ineffective. caught by the police with cocaine valued at $300,000. Wilber has never before been', 'The sentence was unconstitu- convicted of a crime. At trial, Wilber takes tional. the stand in his own defense and the co- caine, which was illegally seized, is used to impeach him. Wilber is convicted and sentenced to life pursuant to the legislature''s guidelines. This was Wilber''s first criminal conviction.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('659', 'mbe', 'mbe_0659', 'prompt_0973', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '14', '', 'The use of the cocaine to impeach is:', 'Harmless error.', 'Proper.', 'Improper.', 'Harmful error.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('66', 'mbe', 'mbe_0066', 'prompt_0349', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '23', '', 'If SK Enterprises'' negligence causes a fire, and there is a lack of adequate in- surance because ofMayor''s negligence, can Girkin sue as a third party benefi- ciary?', 'Yes, if the negligence of the two tortfeasors are proven in court. CONTRACTS m', 'No, because Mayor is an offi- cious intermeddler.', 'No, Girkin did not have the right to receive any duty.', 'No, the agreement of Mayor and GK, Inc. can vitiate any vested rights Girkin had.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('660', 'mbe', 'mbe_0660', 'prompt_0974', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '17', '', 'Minko''s interrogation of Russ can best be characterized as:', 'Necessary under the public safety exception.', 'Coercive.', 'Voluntary.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('661', 'mbe', 'mbe_0661', 'prompt_0975', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '18', '', 'Introduction of Russ'' confession at trial:', 'Can be harmless error.', 'Is per se not harmless error.', 'Violates Equal Protection.', 'Is necessary under the confronta- tion clause.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('662', 'mbe', 'mbe_0662', 'prompt_0976', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '20', '', 'In order to argue on collateral appeal that admission of the confession was erroneous, Russ must show: I. Cause and Prejudice. II. He did not rape Leigh.', 'I only.', 'II only.', 'I and II.', 'I or II. 238 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q Officer J.G. West, while on routine patrol, takes a walk onto the property of Belinda. J.G. looks into Belinda''s barn with his flash- light and sees several machine guns. J.G. then checks Belinda''s dumpster. In it are expended hand grenades. Based on these two pieces of evidence, J.G. obtains a warrant to search Belinda''s barn, home, and office for illegal ammunition. The warrant fails to in- clude the magistrate''s signature. Magistrate Louis had meant to sign i', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('663', 'mbe', 'mbe_0663', 'prompt_0977', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '21', '', 'J.G.''s initial discovery of the machine guns was lawful because:', 'Of the exigency exception.', 'Of the good faith exception.', 'Of the administrative excep- tion.', 'This was not a search.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('664', 'mbe', 'mbe_0664', 'prompt_0978', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '22', '', 'The discovery of the expended hand grenades was lawful because:', 'Of the exigency exception.', 'Defective because it was based on illegally obtained evidence.', 'Not defective because warrants do not need judicial approval.', 'Not defective because of the good faith exception.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('665', 'mbe', 'mbe_0665', 'prompt_0979', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '24', '', 'If J.G. executes the warrant and finds indicia of the illegal ammunition, such is:', 'Admissible, if the warrant is deemed valid.', 'Admissible, if J.G. was in good faith.', 'Admissible, if a statute so al- lows.', 'Inadmissible.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('666', 'mbe', 'mbe_0666', 'prompt_0980', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '27', '', 'Slobog''s initial entry was: the poisonous tree doctrine.', 'Lawful, because of his good', 'Lawful, because of the exigency', 'Lawful, under a doctrine of as-', 'Not lawful.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('667', 'mbe', 'mbe_0667', 'prompt_0981', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '28', '', 'Slobog''s initial observation of the brass case was:', 'Lawful, because of the exigency exception.', 'Lawful, because of the doctrine of assumption of the risk.', 'Lawful, because of the plain view exception.', 'Lawful, because it was not a search. 240 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('668', 'mbe', 'mbe_0668', 'prompt_0982', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '29', '', 'If Sarah is prosecuted for cocaine pos- session, she will likely be:', 'Acquitted, because the cocaine was in her house.', 'Acquitted, because the cocaine was illegally discovered.', 'Convicted, because of Slobog''s good faith.', 'Convicted, because the cocaine would inevitably have been dis- covered.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('669', 'mbe', 'mbe_0669', 'prompt_0983', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '30', '', 'Assume for this question only, that the cocaine belongs to Wayne, Sarah''s live-in boyfriend. Wayne will be:', 'Convicted, because he lacks standing.', 'Convicted, because of Slobog''s good faith.', 'Acquitted, because he has stand- ing.', 'Acquitted, because of the LaFave doctrine.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('67', 'mbe', 'mbe_0067', 'prompt_0350', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '24', '', 'SK Enterprises completes work accord- ing to the sub-contract. Mayor does not pay GK; so GK does not pay SK. SK Enterprises sues Mayor. What is the most likely outcome?', 'Mayor''s damages will be offset by any breach of GK, Inc.', 'Mayor''s damages will be offset by any breach of SK Enterprises.', 'SK Enterprises has suffered a "legal" detriment.', 'SK Enterprises is an incidental beneficiary to the Mayor/GK, Inc. agreement. m CONTRACTS', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('670', 'mbe', 'mbe_0670', 'prompt_0984', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '31', '', 'Assume for this question only, that the cocaine was Wayne''s and that Wayne was invited by Sarah to her apartment to have sex for hire. Assume as well, that this was Wayne''s first visit to Sarah''s apartment. Wayne''s prosecution for cocaine possession will:', 'Succeed, because he lacks stand- ing.', 'Succeed, because he has "un- clean hands."', 'Fail, because he has standing.', 'Fail, because of the LaFave doc- trine.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('671', 'mbe', 'mbe_0671', 'prompt_0985', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '32', '', 'The City of Harzardville has enacted a policy whereby all field officers are subjected to random drug testing. The City has made this policy because of the widespread drug problem. If an officer is found to be using drugs, rehabilitation is offered unless other factors indicate significant involvement with organized crime. The policy is likely:', 'Violates the Double Jeopardy Clause.', 'Violates the Cruel and Unusual Punishment Clause.', 'Violates the Right to Counsel Clause.', 'Lawful, because examination of breath, blood and urine are not searches. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 241 Q Questions 33 — 35 are based on the following facts: Kim is tried on a charge of prostitution. The potential jail term is 6 months. She requests both counsel because she is indigent, and a jury trial. Both requests are denied. She is tried and sentenced to one month in jail.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('672', 'mbe', 'mbe_0672', 'prompt_0986', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '33', '', 'The judge''s denial of counsel was:', 'Not erroneous, because this was a petty offense.', 'Not erroneous, because the of- fense was punishable by under 6 months jail.', 'Not erroneous, because Kim was not indigent.', 'Erroneous.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('673', 'mbe', 'mbe_0673', 'prompt_0987', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '34', '', 'The judge''s denial of a jury trial was:', 'Plain error.', 'Harmless error.', 'Correct.', 'More facts are needed.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('674', 'mbe', 'mbe_0674', 'prompt_0988', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '36', '', 'Mary and Joe agree to commit a bank robbery. Upon entering the First City Bank, they draw their guns and flee with $7,000 of cash. Two days later, they are captured. They are prosecuted in the state for Conspiracy and Robbery. Assuming that the prosecutor proves each element of each crime beyond a reasonable doubt, will their convictions of both crimes stand?', 'Yes, because of the separate sovereign limitation on Double Jeopardy protection.', 'Yes, because Conspiracy and Robbery are distinct offenses un- der Double Jeopardy analysis.', 'No, because each offense does not require proof of an additional fact.', 'No, because Robbery is a lesser included offense of Conspiracy to commit Robbery. 242 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('675', 'mbe', 'mbe_0675', 'prompt_0989', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '38', '', 'Officer Knuckles takes out his brass knuckles and beats Lou to a pulp. Final- ly, Lou admits to having a half-pound of marijuana. Unknown to Knuckles, Officer Smart was on the verge of link- ing Lou to the drug trade in Smalltown. Lou is prosecuted for Possession of a Controlled Substance. Knuckles'' testi- mony and the half-pound of marijuana found by Knuckles are introduced into evidence. Lou''s attorney makes all ap- propriate motions to suppress. These are denied. Lou is convicted, and appeals within the statutory time limit. The prosecution''s best argument, regarding sustaining the conviction, is:', 'I and II only.', 'I only.', 'III and IV only.', 'I and IV only. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 243 Q', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('676', 'mbe', 'mbe_0676', 'prompt_0990', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '39', '', 'A search warrant is issued to search the home of Ebar. The warrant was based on probable cause, properly sworn out before a neutral judge and was specific as to the address of Ebar. The warrant was executed at 1:00 PM on a Thursday afternoon, the day after it was issued. Ebar is detained for 45 minutes, while his house is searched for illegal am- munition and guns. At the time, Ebar''s two friends were also detained. After the discovery of the illegal weaponry, Ebar is arrested. The officers also arrest Ebar''s two friends. After the arrest, the two friends are found to be conceal- ing, on their persons, small amounts of crack cocaine. Which of the following propositions is correct?', 'Ebar''s detention was unlawful, thus the evidence will be sup- pressed.', 'The detention of Ebar''s two friends was unlawful, thus the evidence against Ebar will be suppressed.', 'The detention of Ebar''s two friends was unlawful, thus all evidence against them will be suppressed.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('677', 'mbe', 'mbe_0677', 'prompt_0991', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '40', '', 'Officer Frome swears out an affidavit before a neutral judge. His information is based on the confidential tip of a personally-known informant, that Rick is illegally storing toxic waste in his barn at 123 Main Street. The warrant is issued. In executing the warrant, Frome looks through all large containers in the barn. He finds not only toxic waste, but also several bales of marijuana. Which of the following propositions are cor- rect? I. The search warrant must be based on probable cause. II. The test for probable cause is whether the in- formant was reliable and had a basis for his knowl- edge. III. The seizure of the mari- juana, though outside the scope of the warrant, was probably lawful.', 'I only.', 'I and II only.', 'I and III only.', 'II only. 244 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('678', 'mbe', 'mbe_0678', 'prompt_0992', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '41', '', 'Pawnshops, in the city of Tomato, are closely regulated. The reason for the regulations are that, such are often used to "fence" stolen property, that illegal guns are often sold through pawnshops and that the city has received hundreds of complaints regarding the shops. Among the city ordinances is the re- quirement that, in order to do business, the pawnshop must keep complete records of all chattels received, from whom and also keep records of all chat- tels sold, and to whom such were sold. Moreover, in applying for a business license, they also consent to inspection of the above by the police. The Dirtball, a Tomato City pawn- shop, is inspected by Officer Lemon. He finds several violations, including a weapon violation that subjects the owner of Dirtball to criminal liability. Will a motion to suppress this evidence succeed?', 'No, because of the good faith exception to the warrant require- ment.', 'No, because of the administrative exception.', 'Yes, because a warrant was re- quired.', 'Yes, because of Lemon''s bad faith.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('679', 'mbe', 'mbe_0679', 'prompt_0993', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '42', '', 'Over defense objection, an admission of the defendant is introduced. The admission was taken after police inter- rogation, in violation of Miranda .The defendant is convicted. An appeal is taken. What must the prosecution show, in order to preserve the conviction?', 'That there was no error.', 'That the error was harmless, by a preponderance.', 'That the error was harmless, beyond a reasonable doubt.', 'That the error was harmless, by clear and convincing evidence.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('68', 'mbe', 'mbe_0068', 'prompt_0351', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '25', '', 'Mayor proposes that the business around the sports complex area be painted with a color scheme coordi- nated to that of the Knucklehead''s uni- forms. Because this would mean that Girkin''s restaurant would have to re- paint and change all of the food pack- aging, Girkin is opposed to the Mayor''s renovation plan for the sports complex area. In a 6-to-1 decision, the city coun- cil votes in the Mayor''s plan. Does Girkin have any rights due to the affir- mative nod he received at the party and the reliance upon it?', 'Yes, Girkin has suffered a bar- gained-for legal detriment.', 'Yes, Girkin has reasonably re- lied.', 'Yes, mayor should have known by Girkin''s exclamation that he would rely.', 'No, the communication between the parties has not yet reached a point where vesting could occur in Girkin. Questions 26 - 28 are based on the following facts: Driver injured Ped in an auto accident. Ped hired Axel, an attorney, to represent him. Ped explained to Axel that he was poor and couldn''t afford the needed medical treat- ment. Concerned for his client''s health, Axel wrote into their contract that he would prom- ise to "pay from any settlement with Driver, compensation to any physicians w', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('680', 'mbe', 'mbe_0680', 'prompt_0994', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'property', '43', '', 'Joe is placed under arrest. He is not told what crime he is arrested for. (The crime is extortion.) After being given the Miranda warnings, he says "I don''t want to talk." Two hours later, after the routine booking, another officer interro- gates Joe. Joe is first given Miranda. He waives the warnings, then starts talking. Ultimately, after 45 minutes, Joe makes a highly incriminating statement. Is the statement admissible, in a subsequent criminal trial, for extortion?', 'No, because extortion is not a drug offense.', 'No, because the police initiated the subsequent interrogation.', 'No, because it was, per se, invol- untary. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 245', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('681', 'mbe', 'mbe_0681', 'prompt_0995', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'civil_procedure', '44', '', 'Suzanne is arrested, based on probable cause, that she committed Assault. It is stipulated that there was probable cause. The arrest is made in public. Assault is graded as a misdemeanor in this jurisdiction. The alleged victim of the assault, within 5 minutes of same, is asked whether Suzanne is the perpetra- tor. She responds: "She is definitely the one!" Is this out-of-court identification admissible? In order to help bolster their case, the prosecution sends out a special inves- tigator, to infiltrate Frank''s circle of friends, and thereby to hopefully learn inculpatory facts regarding the rack- eteering charge. The investigator learns several pieces of information, some from the active questioning of Frank, that are relevant to the racketeering charge; and some of which are relevant to other crimes of Frank (for which he has not been charged). Which of the following propositions is the most ac- curate?', 'The use of the investigator was unconstitutional.', 'No information found by the investigator via dialogue with Frank is admissible.', 'No, because this was misleading and overly suggestive.', 'No, because Due Process was denied.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('682', 'mbe', 'mbe_0682', 'prompt_0996', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '46', '', 'Jennifer is charged with Petty Theft. Petty Theft, for first-time offenders, cannot exceed 6 months in jail. Jennifer is a first-time offender. She is 19 years old and cannot afford counsel. Her re- quest to have counsel is denied, on the grounds that she will likely not serve any jail time even if convicted. In fact, she is convicted. No jail time is given. Have Jennifer''s constitutional rights been denied?', 'No, because the potential for jail must be over 6 months, in order for the Right to Counsel to at- tach.', 'Yes, because the conviction might potentially be used if she is charged with another crime.', 'No, because no actual jail time was imposed.', 'Yes, because indigents have an absolute right to the appointment of counsel at "critical stages", such as this.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('683', 'mbe', 'mbe_0683', 'prompt_0997', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '47', '', 'Hilton has gone on a wild killing spree, evidently initiated by the romantic rejection of a woman he loved. Hilton has committed 5 gruesome and bloody Murders. He openly admits to the kill- ing and claims that it was "the devil''s hand that led" him. Hilton''s attorney, pre-trial, moves that Hilton not be tried and be furnished with a psychiatric examination. Under prevailing consti- tutional rules, must the trial judge make such an inquiry?', 'No, this claim should be heard on appeal.', 'Yes, because insane defendants cannot be tried.', 'No, because no facts indicate disorientation or inability to participate in his own defense.', 'Yes, because incompetent defen- dants cannot be tried.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('684', 'mbe', 'mbe_0684', 'prompt_0998', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '48', '', 'Jill and Joyce are tried on a charge of Conspiracy to Distribute Controlled Substances. They are tried together. Joyce takes the stand and argues that Jill used duress in order to obtain her participation. Jill and Joyce are repre- sented by different defense attorneys. Jill''s attorney begins her cross-exami- nation of Joyce. Joyce''s attorney at once objects, arguing that, if such proceeds, Joyce''s Self-incrimination rights will be abrogated. How should the trial judge rule? CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 247', 'For Joyce, because she cannot be compelled to incriminate herself.', 'For Jill, because she has the right to confront adverse witnesses.', 'A mistrial should be declared because two constitutional rights are in direct opposition.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('685', 'mbe', 'mbe_0685', 'prompt_0999', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '49', '', 'The state of Columbia has, by legis- lative enactment, deemed all those convicted of capital crimes and duly sentenced to death, to be executed by dismemberment. Defendant Horace is so sentenced. Which of the follow- ing are relevant inquiries regarding Horace''s challenge to the method of execution? I. Whether other states have similar statutes. II. Whether such is more painful than other meth- ods of execution.', 'I only.', 'II only.', 'I and II.', 'Neither I nor II.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('686', 'mbe', 'mbe_0686', 'prompt_1000', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'constitutional', '52', '', 'Mr. Lifer, a prisoner in State Peniten- tiary, was sound asleep in his prison cell, dreaming of life on the outside, before he killed his fiancé. His dream was interrupted by Mr. Guard, a prison guard at "State Penn." Guard snuck into Lifer''s prison cell and proceeded to search through Lifer''s belongings. Guard woke Lifer up when he found three small balloons of heroin stuck un- derneath Lifer''s bed. Lifer denied any knowledge of the heroin. Guard notified the prison''s warden. Lifer was sent to solitary confinement for three weeks. At trial, Lifer, representing himself, files a motion to suppress the heroin. Are the balloons of heroin admissible against Lifer?', 'No, because Guard had no war- rant to conduct such a search.', 'Locke''s search did not violate the', 'The search was conducted by a', 'Locke''s search, resulting in the discovery of the evidence of cocaine, was "reasonable." CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 249 Questions 53 — 54 are based on the following facts: Mac, a driver for Fed-Excess, was arrested, without warrant, at his place of employment, while waiting for his delivery truck to be filled with packages. Other police officers were simultaneously searching Mac''s home, having been let in by a neighbor who knew where the key was kept. Inside, the officers found and confiscated various i', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('687', 'mbe', 'mbe_0687', 'prompt_1001', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '53', '', 'Should the court grant Mac''s request for the return of his property?', 'No, because the U.S. Marshal was merely trying to bring fur- ther proof to the aid of the Gov- ernment and was acting under the color of his office.', 'No, because the circumstances did not dictate that the U.S. Marshal needed to secure a war- rant.', 'Yes, because the U.S. Marshal acted without sanction of law.', 'Yes, because Mac made a sea- sonal application for the return of his property.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('688', 'mbe', 'mbe_0688', 'prompt_1002', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '54', '', 'Assuming the same facts in question 53, should the government be permitted to use Mac''s property at trial?', 'Yes, because in a prosecution in a State court for a State crime the Fourteenth Amendment does not forbid the admission of evidence obtained by an unreasonable search and seizure.', 'Yes, because the circumstances did not dictate that the U.S. Marshal needed to secure a war- rant.', 'No, because to permit the use of Mac''s property upon trial would result in prejudicial error.', 'No, because the U.S. Marshal acted without sanction of law. Q W Z 0E 250 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q When the officers were inside, Riskay''s attorney arrived, but the officers did not allow him to enter the house, nor speak to Riskay. Riskay demanded to see a search warrant, none was produced and the officers continued to search Riskay''s residence. Irritated with Ris- kay''s repeated pleading to see a search warrant, one of the officers forcibly handcuffed Riskay. The officers ended up searching Riskay''', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('689', 'mbe', 'mbe_0689', 'prompt_1003', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '57', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this question only, that Dinah Gloss'' breath was not excessively minty, nor was she stopped by officer Crumpky for speeding, but was instead stopped because the rear of her car was very low to the ground. Suspecting that something illegal might be in the trunk, Crumpky searched the car and found 12 cases of cigarettes not bearing California tax seals. The car and cigarettes were seized and Gloss was arrested and charged with violation of California law. Crumpky had neither a search nor an arrest warrant. Pursuant to a California statute, a petition for forfei- ture of the automobile was filed. Gloss, by timely objection, seeks dismissal of the forfeiture petition, claiming the evidence was illegally obtained. Will the forfeiture petition be dismissed?', 'Yes, because the seizure was founded upon evidence illegally obtained, since under the par- ticular circumstances Crumpky acted without probable cause.', 'No, because the inventory search exception allows Crumpky to act as he did.', 'No, because the exclusionary rule does not apply to civil for- feitures.', 'Succeed. 252 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q Questions 58 — 62 are based on the following facts: John Palmer, an off-duty police officer, was playing golf one Sunday afternoon. His tee shot on the fourth hole was a bad slice and ended up out-of-bounds, in the backyard of Mrs. Middo. Palmer climbed the hill to Middo''s backyard and began looking for his golf ball, which was easy to do since there was no fence to climb. While looking, Palmer glanced into Middo''s greenhouse and saw several beautifully-manicure', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('69', 'mbe', 'mbe_0069', 'prompt_0352', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '27', '', 'If Doctor brings an action against Axel based on the employment contract with Ped, Axel''s best defense argument would be:', 'The Doctor did not change his position in reliance.', 'The anti-assignment clause is void.', 'Axel relied to his detriment on Ped''s release.', 'The Doctor is an incidental ben- eficiary.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('690', 'mbe', 'mbe_0690', 'prompt_1004', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '58', '', 'Was Palmer''s discovery of the green- house marijuana and the three ouzies lawful?', 'No, because Palmer was off-duty at the time.', 'No, because he had no probable cause to enter Middo''s back- yard.', 'No, because he only had license to look for his golfball and noth- ing else.', 'Yes, because no significant pri- vacy expectation was invaded.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('691', 'mbe', 'mbe_0691', 'prompt_1005', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '59', '', 'The discovery of the hundreds of bag- gies full of marijuana was unlawful because:', 'No significant privacy expecta- tion was invaded.', 'Palmer was presented with an exigent circumstance.', 'Palmer acted in good faith.', 'None of the above.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('692', 'mbe', 'mbe_0692', 'prompt_1006', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '60', '', 'Since the warrant had no signature, it will be deemed:', 'Valid, because the magistrate inadvertently left his signature off.', 'Valid, even if the signature was purposefully left off, as long as the magistrate had all of the facts before him when preparing the warrant.', 'Valid.', 'Defective. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 253 Q', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('693', 'mbe', 'mbe_0693', 'prompt_1007', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '61', '', 'If the warrant is deemed invalid after Officer Palmer executes it, anything found pursuant to that warrant will be:', 'Inadmissible, if Palmer acted on the warrant with the knowledge that it was invalid.', 'Inadmissible.', 'Admissible.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('694', 'mbe', 'mbe_0694', 'prompt_1008', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '62', '', 'If Officer Palmer realizes the warrant is defective because it lacks a signature and executes it despite this knowledge, anything found pursuant to the warrant will be:', 'Admissible.', 'Yes, because the window wasn''t at street level.', 'Admissible, because Palmer''s findings while looking for his golfball in Middo''s backyard were incriminating enough to indicate that other contraband would likely be discovered.', 'None of the above. 254 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Questions 64 — 66 are based on the following facts: Johnny Potseed built himself a huge barn so he could grow pot without any of his neighbors realizing what he was doing. The barn had four walls, all of which were 30 feet high. There were no windows in the walls because Johnny didn''t want anyone to be able to see inside. Johnny didn''t put a roof on the barn so the sun could shine down on his wonderful marijuana buds, making them all the more potent. Act', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('695', 'mbe', 'mbe_0695', 'prompt_1009', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '64', '', 'Since he built the huge barn with four 30 foot walls, will the court find that Johnny Potseed entertained a legitimate expectation of privacy in the premises or area searched?', 'Yes.', 'Yes, because an individual doesn''t have to contemplate the possibility of an aircraft hovering over their structure at an altitude low enough to be able to see inside the structure''s rooftop.', 'Yes, because the construction of the roof successfully denied anyone adjacent to it, or paral- lel to it, a view of the goings on inside of it.', 'No, because the structure of the roof made it like an open or un- covered window. On the basis of the officers'' observations, a search warrant was issued. With warrant in hand, the police forcibly entered the barn and observed close to 100 large marijuana plants, 18-20 feet tall. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 255', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('696', 'mbe', 'mbe_0696', 'prompt_1010', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '66', '', 'Assume that Johnny Potseed had both domestic and farm animals on his property. When the police helicopters hovered 40 feet above the property, the loud noises and vibrations caused by the helicopter''s proximity, caused Johnny''s animals to go into a frenzy, whereby they stampeded wildly around the property. During this frenzy, half of the animals died from shock. In fact, the vibrations were so strong that a glass cabinet full of china crashed to the floor in the kitchen of Johnny''s nearby home. Johnny brings a cause of action against the government for invasion of privacy. Will he prevail?', 'Yes.', 'Yes, but only if the court decides the conduct of the officers in fly- ing at 40 feet above the barn was hazardous to persons or property on the surface.', 'No, because even though damage to persons or property occurred, the officers had a right to fly above Johnny''s property.', 'None of the above. 256 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Questions 67 — 68 are based on the following facts: Bob Dealin put his garbage at the curb in front of his house on Sunday night because the garbage men pick up the neighborhood''s garbage on Monday morning. Bob put his trash in black trash bags and tied them se- curely shut. He used black trash bags and tied them securely shut because he didn''t want anybody to see what was inside the bags nor be able to open them with ease. Bob didn''t want anybody messin', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('697', 'mbe', 'mbe_0697', 'prompt_1011', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '68', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this ques- tion only, that Bob, because he was getting ready to go out of town for a couple days, had placed two opaque trash bags, securely tied, just outside his front door, containing two eight- ounce bricks of hash and other legal personal belongings. Bob was going to take the bags with him on his trip. A police officer, acting on a neighbor''s tip that drug dealing was likely tak- ing place at Bob''s house, walked up to Bob''s front porch and confiscated the two black trash bags. Based on the contents discovered therein, the officer secured a search warrant for Bob''s home and found quantities of hash and drug-dealing paraphernalia inside. Bob files a motion to suppress the evidence at trial. The motion would:', 'Succeed, because the warrant- less search of Bob''s black trash bags violated the Fourth Amend- ment.', 'Succeed, because the black trash bags were being used to store or transport personal possessions.', 'Fail.', 'Both A and B.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('698', 'mbe', 'mbe_0698', 'prompt_1012', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '69', '', 'Sigmund Frodd, experiencing grave fi- nancial difficulties, decided it was time to cheat on his taxes. During 1994, he did many fraudulent things in an effort to get a refund from the government. Frodd shredded all of the documents he used to mastermind his fraudulent activities because he didn''t want any- one to be able to figure out what he had done. Frodd contemplated burning the documents, but decided instead to merely put the shreddings in the trash because the documents had been virtu- ally shredded beyond comprehension. Frodd''s tax return had been red-flagged by the I.R.S. computer and two I.R.S. agents had been sent to Frodd''s house to investigate. The agents searched through garbage bags outside Frodd''s house and found them full of shredded documents. The agents took the garbage bags with them back to their headquar- ters and proceeded to painstakingly piece together the shredded documents. Based upon the pieced-together docu- ments, the I.R.S. was able to make a case of tax fraud against Frodd. A mo- tion to suppress the evidence would:', 'Succeed, because John shredded the documents so no one could read them.', 'Succeed, because the documents in their shredded state were in no way incriminating.', 'Fail. 258 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q', 'Both A and B.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('699', 'mbe', 'mbe_0699', 'prompt_1013', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '70', '', 'During their examination of a damaged package, employees of Federal Excess observed a white powdery substance, originally concealed within eight lay- ers of brown paper and tape. A federal agent was summoned, who removed a trace amount of the white substance, subjected it to a test and determined that it was cocaine. Did the federal agent''s field test constitute an unlawful search or seizure within the meaning of the Fourth Amendment?', 'No, because Prude, a private party, opened the videos herself, revealing the pornographic video boxes.', 'Yes, because a protected posses- sory interest was infringed.', 'Yes, because the field test like- ly destroyed some of the evi- dence.', 'Both B and C.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('7', 'mbe', 'mbe_0007', 'prompt_0034', 'Barbri - Drills and Released Questions - MDR (MBE - 2007)', 'property', '64', '', 'REAL PROPERTY Question 12 State Green has a statute providing as fol- lows: Any judgment properly filed shall, for 10 years from the date of filing, be a lien on the real property then owned or subsequently acquired by any person against whom the judgment is rendered. Oscar conveyed Birchacre, located in State Green, to Allen, who had had a judgment lien recorded against him two years earlier in the county in which Birchacre was located. One year later, Allen conveyed the property to Brenda by general warranty deed. The deed did not mention the lien, but Brenda was aware of it. Two years later, Brenda conveyed Birchacre to Carla by special warranty deed. Carla was not aware of the lien and her deed also made no mention of it. One year after that transaction, Carla conveyed Birchacre to Dave by general warranty deed. Dave’s deed did not mention the lien but Dave was aware of it. The next year, Dave entered into a contract to convey Birchacre to Edgar. Edgar’s title search disclosed the judgment lien against Allen, and Edgar refused to proceed with the transaction because title was not marketable. Dave brought an action against Edgar for specific performance and was denied relief. He then brought an action against Allen, Brenda, and Carla for breach of warranty. Assume for purposes of this question that all transactions concerning Birchacre were prompt- ly and properly recorded, and that the party holding the judgment lien has taken no action as of yet to enforce it. Which parties, if any, will be liable to Dave?', 'Christie owns Blackacre subject to Mari- time Bank’s mortgage, because Christie is a donee.', 'Christie owns Blackacre subject to Mari- time Bank’s mortgage, because Maritime Bank recorded before Christie.', 'Christie owns Blackacre free of Maritime Bank’s mortgage, because Brittany was a bona fide purchaser for value without notice.', 'Christie owns Blackacre free of Maritime Bank’s mortgage, because the bank does not qualify as a bona fide purchaser for value. GO ON TO THE NEXT PAGE', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('70', 'mbe', 'mbe_0070', 'prompt_0353', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '28', '', 'If Doctor brings an action against Grease, Grease will most likely argue:', 'There is no evidence supporting that Doctor changed his position in reliance.', 'There is no evidence supporting a finding that Doctor was either a creditor or donee beneficiary of Grease''s promise to Axel.', 'Grease only made a gratuitous promise.', 'Doctor is an unidentified benefi- ciary. CONTRACTS III', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('700', 'mbe', 'mbe_0700', 'prompt_1014', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '72', '', 'John Dough, a postal worker, opened a suspicious-looking package with a return address from Haight-Asbury Street, in San Francisco. John was hop- ing the package would contain some black-tar hash, given the return address and the fact it was to be delivered to a nearby fraternity house. John took the package home with him and discovered 12 grams of blonde hash instead of black-tar. A little disappointed, John smoked some to get in a better mood. Unfortunately, John became extremely paranoid, realized what he had done was wrong and turned the hash over to the F.B.I. the next morning. The agents brought a prosecution action against the sender. Are John Dough''s actions governed by the Fourth Amendment?', 'No, because John procured the hash for his personal use. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 259', 'Yes, because John is a postal worker.', 'Yes, because John discovered the hash during the course and scope of his government employ- ment.', 'Yes, because John''s search and seizure was done to further a governmental objective.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('701', 'mbe', 'mbe_0701', 'prompt_1015', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '73', '', 'The police were looking for Tom Hoss, a bookmaking kingpin. Two officers drove to Tom''s house, thinking he might be home, to ask him some ques- tions concerning the recent murder of a jockey. They knocked on the door and Tom''s butler answered the door, obvi- ously intoxicated, reeking of martinis. "May we come in?" one of the officers asked. "Yes, you may," the Butler said, with obviously-slurred speech. Did the officers obtain voluntary consent?', 'No, if the officers knew that the butler was drunk.', 'No, if the officers did not know the butler was drunk.', 'Yes.', 'Both A and B.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('702', 'mbe', 'mbe_0702', 'prompt_1016', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '74', '', 'Molly Made works for Weekend Inn, a chain of motels, where she is a part of the room-cleaning staff. While cleaning room 237, Molly discovered a sizeable amount of cocaine on the night stand. Molly quickly walked to the motel''s donut shop where she told Officer Winchell about the cocaine. Officer Winchell followed Molly to room 237 and Molly let Winchell inside the room. Winchell further searched the room and found more cocaine and cocaine para- phernalia. Winchell waited in the room until the guest returned and arrested him for possession of cocaine. Was the consent to search given by Molly Made valid?', 'Yes, if it was after check-out time for room 237.', 'Yes, because a hotel clerk or maid has apparent authority to consent to the search of a guest''s room.', 'No, because a third party cannot give consent.', 'None of the above. 260 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('703', 'mbe', 'mbe_0703', 'prompt_1017', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '76', '', 'The probation condition will be found:', 'Invalid, because an individual cannot sign away his constitu- tional rights.', 'Invalid, because there is implied coercion involved when making a probationer consent in advance to surrender his constitutional rights.', 'Invalid, because such a broadly worded probation condition could lead to invasions of pri- vacy of innocent third parties.', 'Valid.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('704', 'mbe', 'mbe_0704', 'prompt_1018', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '77', '', 'Assume the same facts as question 76, except the above clause is not a condi- tion of Malcolm''s probation. Will the police officer''s search of Malcolm''s home be upheld?', 'No, because the police en- tered Malcolm''s home without a search warrant.', 'No, because Malcolm was out of town and could not consent to the search.', 'Yes, because, under the cir- cumstances, it can be deemed a "regulatory search."', 'Both A and B. Questions 78 — 81 are based on the following facts: Officer Bust posed as a drug purchaser and gained entry into the home of Tommy Dealer, who ran a crack house in south Central L.A. When inside, Bust witnessed several drug sales take place. Tommy was arrested and charged with possession and sale of crack cocaine. Tommy''s lawyer made a motion to suppress the evidence because Bust had gained entry by tricking Tommy.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('705', 'mbe', 'mbe_0705', 'prompt_1019', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '79', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this ques- tion only, that Bust gained entry into Tommy''s home in the manner he did in question 78. Rather than witness drug transactions taking place, Bust took the opportunity while Tommy was in the bathroom to look into closed drawers and envelopes, finding cocaine and cocaine paraphernalia. Tommy''s lawyer made a motion to suppress this evidence. The motion will: 262 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q', 'Fail, because Bust posed as a drug purchaser.', 'Succeed, because Bust looked into closed drawers and enve- lopes while Tommy was in the bathroom.', 'Succeed, because Bust concealed his status as a law enforcement officer.', 'Fail, because Tommy left the room, thus giving Bust implied consent to search the closed drawers and envelopes.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('706', 'mbe', 'mbe_0706', 'prompt_1020', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '80', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this question only, that Officer Bust gained entry into Tommy''s home by pretending to be a telephone technician. Once inside, Bust witnessed drug transactions taking place. Tommy''s lawyer made a motion to suppress the evidence. The motion will:', 'Succeed, because Bust did not represent himself as having an illegal purpose.', 'Fail, because Bust posed as a telephone technician.', 'Fail, because Bust did not make any additional intrusions into the premises of Tommy.', 'Fail. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 263 Q', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('707', 'mbe', 'mbe_0707', 'prompt_1021', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '83', '', 'Officer Jones was driving his patrol car down Sunset Blvd., looking for Tim Johnson, a suspect wearing blue jeans and a lime-green t-shirt, who just robbed a coin store. Jones spotted a man wear- ing blue jeans and a lime-green t-shirt, walking briskly down the boulevard. Jones sped up, got out of his patrol car and accosted the man who he thought was Johnson. With gun drawn, Jones instructed the suspect to put his hands over his head. "What''s this all about?" Johnson asked. "You know what it''s all about," Jones replied. "Don''t tell me you don''t know anything about the coin shop you just robbed, Mr. John- son." "I''m not Johnson." "Yeah, right," replied Jones. Jones searched through the pockets of the suspect and found a vile of cocaine and a metal straw, but didn''t find any coins or any items sold in a coin shop. "Where''d you stash the coins, John- son?" "I''m not Johnson. My name is Howard Limbo. I''m on my way to catch the bus." Jones allowed the suspect to produce his driver''s license. The suspect was not lying, his name was Howard Limbo. Nonetheless, Officer Jones handcuffed Limbo and drove him to the coin shop, hopefully to be identi- fied by the coin shop''s owner.', 'Fail.', 'Succeed, because Officer Jones detained the wrong man.', 'Succeed, because Officer Jones had no reason to search the pock- ets of Howard Limbo.', 'Succeed, because the owner of the coin shop proved Howard Limbo''s innocence.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('708', 'mbe', 'mbe_0708', 'prompt_1022', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '86', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this question only, that Stonelli was home when the officers arrived and they were able to handcuff him without incident, in his living room. Knowing that Stonelli was "armed and dangerous," the officers feared there could be armed and danger- ous associates of Stonelli elsewhere in the house. The officers looked through large mahogany cabinets in the living room as well as closets in an imme- diately adjoining hallway, fearing at- tackers could likely be present. During their search, they found large amounts of cocaine. Stonelli''s lawyer makes a motion to suppress the cocaine. The motion will:', 'Succeed, because the officers did not have probable cause.', 'Succeed, because the police un- necessarily broke down the front door to gain entry.', 'Succeed, because the actions of the officers could have lead to unnecessary violence and en- dangered the lives of innocent people. 266 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q', 'Fail.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('709', 'mbe', 'mbe_0709', 'prompt_1023', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '87', '', 'Assume the same facts as question 86, except the officers searched Stonelli''s entire home, looking under every bed and opening all cabinets, cupboards and closets. During their extensive search, the officers found large amounts of cocaine under the bed in the master bedroom. Stonelli''s lawyer makes a motion to suppress the evidence. The motion will:', 'Succeed, because the officers did not have probable cause to search the entire premises.', 'Succeed, because the officers did not have reasonable suspicion to search the entire premises.', 'Succeed, because the weapons were not discovered "inadver- tently" for the plain view doc- trine to apply, given the fact the officers applied for a warrant indicating weapons.', 'Fail, because the actions of the officers exceeded what was law- ful.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('71', 'mbe', 'mbe_0071', 'prompt_0354', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '29', '', 'Toyco, a recently-established business which manufacture yo-yo''s, entered into a written agreement to purchase all of its requirements of hard plastic for 2 years from Plasco. Toyco and Plasco agreed on a unit price and on delivery and payment terms. The agreement also stated that the parties promised not to assign the contract and that the first four months of payments due to Plasco by Toyco would be made to Credo, a credi- tor of Plasco. Plasco then makes an as- signment of the contract to Loanco for security on a loan. In regards to the non-assignment clause, which state- ment is correct:', 'The assignment is completely ineffective.', 'The non-assignment clause has no legal effect.', 'Plasco''s assignment is a breach ofcontract with Toyco, but there is still an effective transfer to Loanco.', 'It is void as against public policy. m CONTRACTS Questions 30 and 31 are based on the following facts: Martha owns a small interior decorating business. Because of the record recession, Martha finds herself in financial difficulty. In fact, Martha becomes $10,000 behind in payments to Design Factory, who supplies Martha with her materials. Martha stumbles into a great offer with Swingin Star to re- decorate her home. Martha enters into a con- tract with Swingin Star to redecorate her home and Sw', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('710', 'mbe', 'mbe_0710', 'prompt_1024', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '88', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this question only, that the officers applied for a war- rant to search Stonelli''s house for weap- ons and stolen property, but the warrant as signed by the magistrate authorized only to search for stolen property. The officers executing the warrant did not find any stolen property but found the weapons in plain view. Stonelli''s lawyer made a motion to suppress the weapons. The motion will: 267 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Questions 89 — 91 are based on the following facts: III. Reasonable.', 'I and II only.', 'III only.', 'I, II, and III.', 'II and III only. Three officers executed a valid warrant to search for drugs in a neighborhood bar known for drug use. Once lawfully inside the bar, the officers began searching customers of the bar for drugs. The officers searched pockets, purses and wallets for any indicia of drugs.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('711', 'mbe', 'mbe_0711', 'prompt_1025', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '89', '', 'The search of the customers was:', 'Lawful, because people who frequent a bar known for drug use are likely to have drugs on their person.', 'Lawful, because the officers searched pockets, purses and wallets.', 'Unlawful, because the officers had no probable cause to arrest the customers.', 'Unlawful because the search warrant only allowed the officers to search the bar, not the personal effects of the customers.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('712', 'mbe', 'mbe_0712', 'prompt_1026', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '91', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this ques- tion only, that the officers temporar- ily detained the customers of the bar while they conducted their search of the premises. Such an action would be deemed: I. Coercive. II. A violation of the Due Process Clause. III. Unconstitutional.', 'I only.', 'II and III only.', 'I, II and III.', 'None of the above. I. A Fourth Amendment right violation. II. An invasion of privacy. 268 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE Q Questions 92 — 93 are based on the following facts: Police officers were sent to a downstairs apartment where a man lay wounded from a gunshot wound. The officers determined that the bullet was shot from the upstairs apartment as there was a hole in the ceiling of the downstairs apartment consistent with a bullet hole. The officers lawfully entered the upstairs apartment to search for t', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('713', 'mbe', 'mbe_0713', 'prompt_1027', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '92', '', 'Was the officer''s conduct concerning the CD player lawful?', 'Yes, because the officers lawfully entered the apartment.', 'Yes, because recording the se- rial numbers did not constitute a seizure.', 'Yes, because the presence of an expensive CD player in an ill-furnished apartment in a poverty-stricken neighborhood was a proper basis for reasonable suspicion.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('714', 'mbe', 'mbe_0714', 'prompt_1028', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '93', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this question only, that one of the officers found and seized a box of pen guns in plain view. Later, at the station, it was determined that these pen guns, which look exactly like ballpoint pens to the naked eye, were in fact contraband (weapons). Was the seizure of the pen guns lawful? I. Yes, because they were discovered in plain view. II. Yes, because they were later determined to be contraband. III. No, because they were later determined to be contraband.', 'I', 'I and II', 'III', 'None of the above. CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 269 Q _,Lu Qs Z_ p ..''"'' LIJ 2 (.) Fe0 Oa Questions 94 — 98 are based upon the following facts: Assume the following facts for the purpose of this question only: Johnny Boy ran a red light late one night and was pulled over by Officer 0'' Hair. Johnny was asked to step out of the car because his pupils were dilated and he was talking extremely fast. O''Hair suspected cocaine use. Johnny failed a field sobriety test and was taken back to the police station. The', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('715', 'mbe', 'mbe_0715', 'prompt_1029', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '94', '', 'Should Johnny''s incriminating state- ments be suppressed?', 'No, because the questioning was consistent with the Fifth Amend- ment.', 'Yes, because when Johnny requested that none of his state- ments be put in writing or tape- recorded, the officers should have realized that Johnny didn''t understand that his statements could be used as evidence even if not written down or recorded.', 'Yes, because Johnny''s waiver of his Miranda rights was con- ditioned.', 'Yes, because Johnny indicated he would not make a written statement outside the presence of counsel.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('716', 'mbe', 'mbe_0716', 'prompt_1030', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '95', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this ques- tion only, that nine police officers had lawfully entered Johnny Boy''s house at 3:00 a.m. and questioned him in his own bedroom, without reading him the Miranda warnings. During this ques- tioning session, Johnny made several incriminating statements. Should the officers have read Johnny Boy his Mi- randa rights?', 'No, because he was not being questioned at the police station.', 'No, because a suspect cannot be in custody in his own home.', 'Yes, because there were nine officers in Johnny Boy''s bed- room.', 'Yes, because a reasonable person would have believed this was an interrogation.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('717', 'mbe', 'mbe_0717', 'prompt_1031', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '96', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this ques- tion only, that Johnny Boy accepted an invitation from a police officer to come down to the police station for question- ing, but was not under arrest. Would Johnny be deemed to be in custody?', 'Yes, because the questioning took place at the police station. 270 CRIMINAL PROCEDURE', 'Yes, because Johnny was invited by a police officer.', 'No, unless the invitation ap- peared to be a kind of offer that could not be reasonably refused.', 'No, because Johnny was not under arrest.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('718', 'mbe', 'mbe_0718', 'prompt_1032', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '97', '', 'Assume for the purpose of this ques- tion only, that Johnny Boy was pulled over by Officer 0'' Hair under suspicion of drunk driving. Officer O''Hair pro- ceeded to ask Johnny Boy a series of questions, trying to ascertain whether he had been drinking, how much he had been drinking and when he had his last drink. Would Officer O''Hair''s ques- tioning of Johnny Boy be considered custodial interrogation?', 'Yes, because Officer O''Hair is a police officer.', 'Yes, because Officer O''Hair detained Johnny Boy.', 'No, because Johnny Boy was not arrested.', 'No, but only if O''Hair knew Johnny had been drinking.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('719', 'mbe', 'mbe_0719', 'prompt_1033', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '98', '', 'Bob Youbanks, on a hot night in the summer of 1994, engaged by himself in a series of armed robberies in the state of Columbia. Youbanks, a home- less entrepreneur, sold wedding dresses at the local swap meet every weekend and, low on inventory, he decided to rob five bridal supply shops. The victims, in each case, were the business owners who were forced to hand over three designer wedding gowns at gun point. At two of the stores, Youbanks fired his handgun, severely injuring four custom- ers. In fact, one customer was purpose- fully shot and killed by Youbanks. Columbia state law provides that when a defendant pleads guilty, "the court must cause the punishment to be de- termined by a jury" and may "cause witnesses to be examined, to ascertain the character of the offense." Before the matter came to trial, the court determined that Youbanks was indigent. He was provided with a public defender. A week later, at his arraign- ment, Youbanks pleaded guilty to all six offenses. Youbanks was found guilty of all counts, based on credible eye-wit- ness testimony, and sentenced to death by electrocution. On automatic appeal, it was discovered, as indicated in the record, that the judge did not ask Youbanks any questions concerning his plea, and Youbanks did not in any way address the court. Can Youbank''s conviction be reversed? CRIMINAL PROCEDURE 271', 'Yes, because he was denied due process of law.', 'No, because he was found guilty based on credible eye-witness testimony.', 'No, because he pleaded guilty.', 'No, because there cannot be reversible error when the defen- dant admits guilt.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('72', 'mbe', 'mbe_0072', 'prompt_0355', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '30', '', 'Martha''s contract with Swingin Star makes Design Factory:', 'An incidental beneficiary.', 'A creditor beneficiary.', 'A donee beneficiary.', 'An intended beneficiary.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('720', 'mbe', 'mbe_0720', 'prompt_1034', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '100', '', 'Robert Rotten was indicted on two felony counts of bookmaking. After negotiations, Rotten''s attorney was able to persuade the district attorney to allow his client to plead to a lesser-in- cluded offense, which carried a maxi- mum sentence of one year in prison, upon conviction. The district attorney also agreed to make no recommenda- tions as to length of sentence. Rotten subsequently entered his plea of guilty to the lesser offense. Rotten explained to the sentencing judge that his plea was voluntary and he understood all implications of pleading guilty. Rotten''s plea was accepted and a date for sentencing was set. Later, Rotten''s attorney had discovered that evidence to be used against his client had been illegally seized, and immediately moved to withdraw the guilty plea. The motion was denied.', 'Yes, because the earlier plea did not irrevocably waive Rotten''s federal constitutional right to a trial.', 'No, because the judge was not influenced by the prosecutor''s recommendation.', 'No, because a plea cannot rest on a promise or agreement of the prosecutor.', '(1991), it was held that there is no seizure unless there is actual restraint or a submission to police authority. There was none here. Thus, C, which includes choice I, II and III is the correct answer. A is the best answer. Note: The call of this question is phrased in the negative. A is clearly worse because the Bail Clause was never made state-appli- cable; thus, it is a very bad answer. B, though amorphous, may be correct. Due Process might offer Marie some protec- tion. Since there was imp', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('721', 'mbe', 'mbe_0721', 'prompt_1035', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '1', '', 'Petunia sued Daisy for injuries that she suffered when Daisy''s bicycle collided with hers. The issue is whether Daisy was riding her bicycle on the correct side of the roadway. Petunia wants to introduce a photograph that shows that Daisy was riding her bicycle on the wrong side of the road. Daisy objects to the introduction of the photograph. The Court will rule that the photograph is:', 'Admissible, if there is testimony offered showing the photograph to be an accurate representation of the scene of the accident.', 'Admissible, because Daisy can cross-examine Petunia as to the accuracy of the photograph.', 'Inadmissible, unless the photog- rapher testifies to the accuracy of the photograph.', 'Inadmissible, unless the photo- graph was taken by an investiga- tive agent at the accident scene. Questions 2 - 5 are based on the following facts. Mistress was tried for the October 31st murder of Wife.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('722', 'mbe', 'mbe_0722', 'prompt_1036', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '2', '', 'Mistress calls Frieda as her first witness to testify to Mistress'' reputation in the community as a "peaceful woman." The Court will rule the testimony:', 'Admissible, as it tends to prove that Mistress is believable.', 'Admissible, as it tends to prove Mistress is innocent.', 'Inadmissible, because Mistress has not yet testified.', 'Inadmissible, because reputa- tion evidence is inadmissible to prove one''s character.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('723', 'mbe', 'mbe_0723', 'prompt_1037', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '3', '', 'Mistress calls Westley to testify that on October 30, Mistress said she was leaving that day to visit relatives in another state. The Court will rule the testimony:', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not fall- ing within any exception.', 'Inadmissible, because it is irrel- evant.', 'Admissible, as non-hearsay.', 'Admissible, because it is a decla- ration of Mistress'' present state of mind. EVIDENCE 305', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('724', 'mbe', 'mbe_0724', 'prompt_1038', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '4', '', 'Mistress calls Wanda to testify to her alibi. On cross-examination, the prosecutor asks, "Aren''t you Mistress'' Aunt?" The Court will rule the ques- tion:', 'Improper, because the question goes beyond the scope of direct examination.', 'Improper, because the question is irrelevant.', 'Proper, as it tends to prove bias.', 'Proper, because a relative cannot testify as to another relative''s reputation.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('725', 'mbe', 'mbe_0725', 'prompt_1039', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '6', '', 'Joe was robbed by a man carrying an unusual psychedelic-painted gun. Sam was arrested and charged with armed robbery. At trial, the prosecution seeks to offer evidence that Sam robbed Pete with the same gun. The Court will rule the evidence:', 'The question goes beyond the scope of direct examination.', 'The probative value is out- weighed by the prejudicial ef- fect.', 'It is a leading question.', 'Prior jury service renders Willy incompetent as a witness. 306 EVIDENCE Q m 5 mz C) m Questions 7 - 10 are based on the following facts. Paul sues Joe''s Bar and Grille for personal injuries he received in an automobile acci- dent caused by Pete. Pete had been a patron of Joe''s Bar and Grille. Paul claims that Pete was permitted to drink too much liquor at the bar before the accident.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('726', 'mbe', 'mbe_0726', 'prompt_1040', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '8', '', 'Joe''s Bar and Grill calls Pete as a wit- ness, expecting him to testify that he was sober when he left. To their sur- prise, Pete testified that he may have had a little too much to drink that night. Joe''s Bar now wants to confront Pete with a statement he made at his depo- sition that he was sober when leaving that night. Paul objects. Which of the following is the most likely ruling by the court regarding Pete''s statement in his deposition?', 'It is inadmissible, because one cannot impeach his own wit- ness.', 'It is inadmissible, because it is hearsay.', 'It may be used to refresh Pete''s memory.', 'It is admissible to impeach Pete and as evidence that Pete was sober.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('727', 'mbe', 'mbe_0727', 'prompt_1041', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '9', '', 'Paul then offers evidence that, after the accident, the owner of Joe''s Bar and Grille put into effect a rule limiting all patrons to 2 drinks per hour. The Court will rule that this evidence is:', 'Admissible, to show that Joe''s Bar was negligent on previous occasions.', 'Admissible, to show that Joe''s Bar knew precautionary mea- sures were needed. EVIDENCE 307 iu', 'Admissible, because subsequent measures are binding on Joe''s Bar and Grille.', 'Inadmissible, because subse- quent remedial measures are not admissible to prove culpable conduct.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('728', 'mbe', 'mbe_0728', 'prompt_1042', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '12', '', 'The prosecuting attorney wants to introduce a letter that Angelo wrote to Mario before the "hit." Angelo wrote: "Mario, we should have done it sooner, I''ve always wanted to be part of a fam- ily." It was signed, "Angelo."', 'Provide the Court with the origi- nal letter.', 'Show evidence of the authentic- ity of the letter.', 'Show why the letter is relevant.', 'Have an expert witness testify that Angelo wrote the letter.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('729', 'mbe', 'mbe_0729', 'prompt_1043', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '14', '', 'Poultry sues Duck Co. for injuries sus- tained when Poultry''s truck collided with Duck''s truck. Duck''s manager prepared a report of the accident, at the request of the company''s attorney. Dur- ing discovery, Poultry demanded that the report be produced. Will the Court rule for the production of the report?', 'Inadmissible, because the best evidence is Sneak''s notes. 309 EVIDENCE Q ("3zWn5 W', 'No, because the report contains hearsay.', 'No, because the report is self- serving.', 'Yes, because business reports are not privileged. Card calls Ace to testify to admissions made by Duce in the conference. Duce objects. The Court should rule that Ace''s testimony is:', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('73', 'mbe', 'mbe_0073', 'prompt_0356', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '31', '', 'If Martha refuses to complete the work for Swingin Star and Design Factory sues Swingin Star for the amount due, Design Factory will most likely:', 'Lose, because Martha must pay.', 'Lose, because Design Factory is subject to the defense of the fail- ure of Martha''s performance.', 'Win, because the rights were vested.', 'Win, because no defenses are applicable in this fact situation.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('730', 'mbe', 'mbe_0730', 'prompt_1044', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '15', '', 'Pat sued David for damage to his home. The damage resulted from a chemical explosion from an experiment David was conducting in the garage. A relevant fact in Pat''s lawsuit is the magnitude of the explosion. On direct examination, Pat was asked if he re- membered the explosion. He stated, "I recall my daughter, Ella, running out from her bedroom and screaming that the bedroom windows had just shat- tered." Ella is 14-years-old.', 'Admissible, even if Ella is avail- able to testify.', 'Inadmissible, because Ella is available to testify.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception.', 'Inadmissible, because Ella is a child.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('731', 'mbe', 'mbe_0731', 'prompt_1045', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '17', '', 'Farmer John sues Jolly Rancher for damages done to Farmer''s crops by Rancher''s cow. Farmer offers testimony that he looked up Rancher''s telephone number in the telephone book. Farmer proceeded to call that number and a voice answered saying, "This is Jolly Rancher." Farmer then asked, "Was it your cow that trampled my sweet peas?" The voice replied, "Yes." The Court should rule the testimony:', 'Admissible, because Rancher identified himself as the speaker, coupled with the accuracy of the telephone book, and the phone transmission system, furnishes sufficient authentication.', 'Admissible, because judicial notice may be taken of telephone books.', 'Inadmissible, unless Farmer can prove he is familiar with Rancher''s voice. 310 EVIDENCE Q 1-15 mzo m', 'Inadmissible, unless Rancher has been given the opportunity to admit or deny whether the conversation took place or not.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('732', 'mbe', 'mbe_0732', 'prompt_1046', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '19', '', 'Porter then calls Valor to testify that David''s car did run the red light. The Court should rule that Valor''s testimony is:', 'Admissible, because Valor''s testimony is relevant to material issues.', 'Admissible, because Porter was surprised by Witt''s testimony.', 'Inadmissible, because Porter is bound by Witt''s testimony.', 'Inadmissible, because Porter cannot impeach his own wit- ness.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('733', 'mbe', 'mbe_0733', 'prompt_1047', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '20', '', 'On cross-examination Valor is asked by David''s attorney if he was "high" at the time he witnessed the collision. Valor replied, "No, I have never touched alcohol or drugs in my life." David''s attorney now seeks to introduce testi- mony of Tattle that Valor had been high from a joint he smoked at a party 18 months previous. The Court will rule Tattle''s testimony: 311 EVIDENCE Q bjz Wci 5 W', 'Inadmissible, because a witness cannot be impeached by specific acts.', 'Inadmissible, because the ques- tion of whether Valor was high or not is a collateral matter.', 'Admissible, to show Valor is not truthful.', 'Admissible, to impeach Valor as to his lack of memory. Questions 21 - 25 are based on the following fact pattern. Driver hit and injured Jogger while Jogger was jogging across a marked crosswalk. There were several witnesses at the scene of the accident. Jogger alleges that Driver was under the influence when he struck him.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('734', 'mbe', 'mbe_0734', 'prompt_1048', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '21', '', 'Jogger''s counsel calls Polly who was a passenger in Driver''s car along with her sister Dolly. Polly testifies that just before the accident Dolly yelled out, "Be careful, there is a man in the crosswalk." The Court will rule this testimony:', 'Inadmissible, because the state- ment preceded the accident.', 'Inadmissible, because Dolly is available as a witness.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception.', 'Admissible, as an excited utter- ance.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('735', 'mbe', 'mbe_0735', 'prompt_1049', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '22', '', 'Jogger''s counsel next calls Policeman who testifies, "Polly, the passenger, said, ''We hit Jogger while he was in the crosswalk''. Driver was present but remained silent." The Court will rule this testimony:', 'Admissible, because Polly''s statement is imputed to Driver.', 'Admissible, as a party admis- sion. 312 EVIDENCE m 5 m C)', 'Admissible, as a party admis- sion.', 'Admissible, but only if the judge gives an instruction that the statement only goes to liability, not damages.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('736', 'mbe', 'mbe_0736', 'prompt_1050', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '23', '', 'Jogger''s counsel desires to introduce Jogger''s testimony that the day after the accident Jogger said, "My hip must be fractured, it is killing me." The Court will rule the testimony:', 'Inadmissible, because it is self- serving.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception.', 'Admissible, as a statement de- scribing the declarant''s physical condition.', 'Admissible, to prove Jogger''s hip was fractured.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('737', 'mbe', 'mbe_0737', 'prompt_1051', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '25', '', 'On the day after the accident, Polly passenger wrote a letter to Uncle Solly describing the details of the accident. At trial, Polly cannot remember some details of the accident. Jogger''s coun- sel wants to show Polly her letter over the objection of Driver''s attorney. The Court should:', 'Allow the letter under past recol- lection recorded.', 'Allow the letter under present recollection refreshed.', 'Disallow the letter because it was written one day after the accident.', 'Disallow the letter because the letter is self-serving.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('738', 'mbe', 'mbe_0738', 'prompt_1052', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '26', '', 'Candy sues Bill for defamation. Candy alleges that Bill has told all her friends and employer that she once was a "call girl." At trial, Bill calls Officer Smith to testify that 8 years ago Candy was arrested for prostitution. The Court will rule that Smith''s testimony is:', 'Admissible, because truth is a defense. EVIDENCE 313 Q L(. I. 51Z L L Io 5 W', 'Admissible, because character itself is at issue. Questions 28 - 29 are based on the following facts.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay.', 'Inadmissible, because the preju- dicial effect outweighs any pro- bative value. Push sues Shove for injuries Push received in an auto accident. Push alleges that Shove was exceeding the speed limit and was weav- ing over the center line.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('739', 'mbe', 'mbe_0739', 'prompt_1053', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '27', '', 'Felicia, a divorcee who despises her ex-husband, Stan, reads in the paper that he is being tried for fraudulent stock transactions. Felicia remembers that while she was married to Stan, he had explained the scheme to her. Felicia wants to testify for the prosecution. Stan objects. Will the Court allow Felicia to testify?', 'Yes, because once the marriage is dissolved the privilege no longer exists.', 'Yes, because Stan committed a crime.', 'No, because it was a confidential communication made during marriage.', 'No, because Felicia is only doing it out of spite for Stan.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('74', 'mbe', 'mbe_0074', 'prompt_0357', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '32', '', 'Allegro was an unknown struggling songwriter. One evening at a Mozart concert, he meets and falls in love with Soprano. Soprano marvels at the talent hIm, pays the rent, ana COOKS anu cleans, so he can continue his musical work. Allegro is so grateful that he writes Soprano a beautiful song prom- ising to give her 20% of any future roy- alties he receives from his music. One day Allegro is humming a new tune walking down Broadway. Because he is not paying any attention, he is hit by a taxi and dies instantly. One week later, his estate enters into a contract with Blast Records and his song, titled "To Soprano", becomes a #2 hit. Also, it is selected as a movie theme for Madonna''s new movie. Soprano brings a cause of action against Allegro''s es- tate to recover her 20% of the royal- ties. What result will most likely hap- pen?', 'She will win because the assign- ment is enforceable.', 'She will win because her house-keeping constitutes valu- able consideration.', 'She will lose because future con- tract claims are not assignable.', 'She will lose because Allegro made an illusory promise. Questions 33 - 35 are based on the following facts: The Van Gogh museum, located in Wyo- ming, was famous for its collection of rare silver spoons. The museum was very inter- ested in acquiring the spoon used by Henry lli:1l:l W lUI VV 11l~1Il:1, d WVllu-J.11DVvlCO.-al.-1, to acquire the spoon. Whistler has an incred- ible reputation with the museums locally as well as abroad. Fork, who owns the Henry VIn spoon, offers to sell it to Whis', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('740', 'mbe', 'mbe_0740', 'prompt_1054', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '28', '', 'Walt is called as an eyewitness by Push. Walt testifies, on direct examination, that Shove was wearing a red jacket at the time of the accident. Shove''s coun- sel calls Wally to testify that Shove''s jacket was green. Wally''s testimony is:', 'Admissible, as tending to prove a material fact.', 'Admissible, as bearing on Walt''s truthfulness.', 'Inadmissible, because Walt''s ca- pacity to observe is not at issue.', 'None of the above.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('741', 'mbe', 'mbe_0741', 'prompt_1055', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '29', '', 'Shove testifies that he was only driv- ing 30 m.p.h. Counsel for Push does not cross-examine Shove as to speed. Instead, he calls Officer to testify that Shove stated to Officer that he was driving 40 m.p.h.. The Court will find Officer''s testimony:', 'Inadmissible, because it is hear- say not within any exception.', 'Inadmissible, because no foun- dation was laid. 314 EVIDENCE', 'Admissible, as a prior inconsis- tent statement.', 'Admissible, as an admission.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('742', 'mbe', 'mbe_0742', 'prompt_1056', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '30', '', 'Hilda is on trial for the murder of Betty. Hilda does not plan on taking the stand. However, Hilda calls Lionel to testify that Hilda has a reputation for non- violence. Will the Court allow Lionel''s testimony?', 'Yes, as an offer of evidence to show Hilda''s good character.', 'No, because character evidence is not admissible to support a defendant''s claim she is innocent of the crime.', 'No, because Hilda has not yet taken the stand.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('743', 'mbe', 'mbe_0743', 'prompt_1057', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '32', '', 'In order to establish a breach of standard of care owed to her by Abel and Baker, Client:', 'Must have a legal expert from the same locality testify that de- fendant''s conduct was a breach.', 'Must have a legal expert from the same state testify that defen- dants'' conduct was a breach.', 'Can rely on the application of the jurors'' common knowledge as to whether there was a breach.', 'Did not contribute to her own injuries.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('744', 'mbe', 'mbe_0744', 'prompt_1058', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '34', '', 'Paul sued Hotel 6 for injuries he sus- tained in a fall in the hotel lobby. The lobby floor was covered with tile. The evidence was that the lobby floor had been waxed approximately an hour before Paul slipped on it and, although the wax had dried, there appeared to be excessive dried wax caked on several tiles. Hotel 6 denied Paul''s claim that it was negligent. Hotel 6 offered proof that the week before Paul''s fall, at least 11,000 people had walked across the lobby floor without incident. The trial judge should rule the evidence:', 'Admissible, because it tends to prove that Paul did not use rea- sonable care. 316 EVIDENCE m m 0', 'Admissible, because it tends to prove that Hotel 6 was careful in maintaining the floors.', 'Admissible, because it proves that no dangerous condition ex- isted.', 'Inadmissible, because it does not bear on the issue of Hotel 6''s exercise of due care on this occasion.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('745', 'mbe', 'mbe_0745', 'prompt_1059', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '36', '', 'Cam sued Dan for injuries suffered by Cam when their automobiles collided. At trial, Cam offers into evidence a properly authenticated letter from Dan that says, "Your claim seems too high, but because I might be found at fault, I''m prepared to offer you half of what you ask." The letter is:', 'Jamie first proves that Sarah was an employee of Powell Corpo- ration at the time she wrote her statement, and that the statement concerned a matter within the scope of her employment.', 'Jamie produces independent evidence that Adam was not wearing his glasses.', 'Sarah is shown to be beyond the court''s subpoena power.', 'Inadmissible, because Dan''s statement was made in an effort to settle a claim.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('746', 'mbe', 'mbe_0746', 'prompt_1060', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '37', '', 'Jamie''s counsel offers evidence that, shortly after the accident, Powell Cor- poration put a speed governor on the truck involved in the accident. The evidence is:', 'Admissible, as an admission.', 'Admissible, as a declaration against interest.', 'Inadmissible, as a subsequent remedial repair.', 'Inadmissible, because it would lead to the inference that Powell Corporation was at fault.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('747', 'mbe', 'mbe_0747', 'prompt_1061', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '39', '', 'Powell''s counsel seeks to have Officer testify that while he was investigating the accident Jamie told him , "This was my fault." The evidence is:', 'Admissible, since it is exempted from the hearsay rule.', 'Admissible, as an exception to the hearsay rule.', 'Admissible, as a declaration against interest.', 'Inadmissible, since it is hearsay not within any exception. 318 EVIDENCE m F7 m', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('748', 'mbe', 'mbe_0748', 'prompt_1062', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '40', '', 'Birdie was arrested and charged with illegally driving a stolen vehicle across the state line. Evidence was presented that he drove the car from New York to Chicago. The judge took judicial notice of the fact that it was impossible to drive from New York to Chicago without crossing the state line. The judge''s tak- ing of judicial notice:', 'Satisfied the prosecutor''s burden of production on that point.', 'Shifted the burden to Birdie, to present evidence in rebuttal.', 'Shifted the burden on Birdie, to prove that he did not drive from New York to Chicago.', 'Conclusively proved the point, that to drive from New York to Chicago, state lines were crossed.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('749', 'mbe', 'mbe_0749', 'prompt_1063', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'torts', '42', '', 'At the trial of Bill for a murder that occurred in Newport, the prosecution called Wally, who testified that he saw Bill kill the victim. Bill believed that Wally was 200 miles away in San Di- ego, engaged in a narcotics deal with George, a mutual acquaintance, on the day in question. On cross-examination by Bill, Wally was asked whether he had, in fact, been in San Diego purchas- ing narcotics on that date. Wally refused to answer the question on self-incrimi- nation grounds. The judge ordered Wally to answer the question, or his testimony would be stricken from the record. The order to testify or have the testimony stricken can best be supported on the basis that:', 'Wally had not been charged with any crime and, thus, could claim no privilege.', 'Admissible, provided that the privilege prevented adequate hoax resulted in conviction of cross-examination. Abel. EVIDENCE 319 Q W .)z Wa 5 W', 'The public interest in allowing an accused to defend himself outweighs the interest of a non- party witness in the privilege.', 'The trial record does not estab- lish that Wally''s answer could be incriminating.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('75', 'mbe', 'mbe_0075', 'prompt_0358', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '33', '', 'Fork tenders the spoon to Van Gogh. She then requests payment of $50,000. Van Gogh refuses, stating that there is no obligation. Fork brings a breach of contract action. What will the most likely result be?', 'Fork will lose because the per- formance is too personal.', 'Fork will lose because there is no existing privity.', 'Fork will win because she paid valuable consideration for the assignment.', 'Fork will win because the duty was delegable. CONTRACTS III', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('750', 'mbe', 'mbe_0750', 'prompt_1064', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '43', '', 'Dick is being prosecuted for burglary. At trial, Dick testifies on his own be- half, denying that he had committed the burglary. On cross-examination, the prosecution asks Dick whether he had been convicted ten years ago for burglary. The question of the earlier burglary conviction is:', 'Proper, if the court determined that its probative value out- weighed its prejudicial effect.', 'Proper, because the prosecutor is entitled to make such an in- quiry.', 'Improper, because it is character evidence.', 'Improper, because the conviction is ten years old, and the defense must have been given notice of its use prior to trial.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('751', 'mbe', 'mbe_0751', 'prompt_1065', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '44', '', 'In a suit attacking the validity of a trust executed ten years ago, Plaintiff alleges mental incompetency of Jack, the set- tlor, and offers into evidence a properly authenticated affidavit of Henry, Jack''s brother. The affidavit stated that Henry had observed Jack closely over a period of a month, that Jack had engaged in instances of unusual behavior (which were described), and that Jack''s appear- ance had changed from being neat, alert and aware, to disordered and absent- minded. The judge should rule Henry''s affidavit:', 'Inadmissible, as opinion.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay, not within any exception.', 'Admissible, since the declaration was the best evidence that the statement was made.', 'Admissible, as an official state- ment.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('752', 'mbe', 'mbe_0752', 'prompt_1066', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '45', '', 'Allen and Neal were arrested for armed robbery. They were taken to the police station and placed in an interrogation room. After the police officer gave them their Miranda warning, Allen said, "Look, Neal planned the whole thing, we robbed the place, and I was dumb enough to follow along." Neal said nothing. Neal was then taken to another room, and a full confession was obtained from Allen. 320 EVIDENCE If Neal is brought to trial for armed robbery, the fact that Neal failed to ob- ject to Allen''s statement and remained silent, is:', 'Admit the invoice, only because it is a record of regularly con- ducted business activity.', 'Admissible, because a state- ment of a participant in a crime is admissible against another participant.', 'Inadmissible, because it is hear- say.', 'Inadmissible, because, under the circumstances, there was no duty for Neal to respond.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('753', 'mbe', 'mbe_0753', 'prompt_1067', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '47', '', 'James, while crossing the street at the intersection of Nutwood and State Col- lege, was hit by a car driven by Ginger. James sued Ginger for his injuries. At trial, James called Officer Sparky to testify that, ten minutes after the accident, a bus driver stopped and said to him, "Officer, a few minutes ago I saw a hit and run accident at Nutwood and State College involving a red truck and a pedestrian. I followed the truck to McPlace for Ribs, on Third Avenue." Sparky''s testimony should be:', 'Admissible, as a present sense impression.', 'Admissible, as a statement of perception.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception. EVIDENCE 321 z a', 'Inadmissible, because it is irrel- Questions 48-50 are based on the evant. following facts: Raymond, a Hallmark salesman, was a guest at the Holiday Hotel in Arizona. After check- ing into the hotel, he went to the elevator on the lobby level floor to proceed to his room. When the elevator doors opened, he stepped into the elevator shaft, unaware that the elevator car itself had not descended to the lobby. Raymond fell 10 feet to the elevator shaft floor. The elevator actually was out of service', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('754', 'mbe', 'mbe_0754', 'prompt_1068', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '48', '', 'Raymond''s proposed testimony re- garding his statement to the concierge should be:', 'Admissible, because the state- ment contradicts Raymond''s claim that he had no prior in- jury.', 'Admissible, because the state- ment relates to Raymond''s medi- cal condition which is in issue.', 'Inadmissible, because the state- ment is protected from disclosure by the physician-patient privi- lege.', 'Excluded, because Raymond is not qualified as an expert to determine his own medical con- dition.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('755', 'mbe', 'mbe_0755', 'prompt_1069', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '49', '', 'Assume for this question, that Raymond intends to offer testimony that he never complained of knee pain prior to his fall. Raymond''s proposed testimony re- garding the absence of prior complaints of knee pain is:', 'Admissible, because the state- ment is reliable.', 'Admissible, because the state- ment relates to a condition per- ceivable by a lay person.', 'Admissible, because Raymond may give an opinion as to his own diagnosis and required treat- ment.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('756', 'mbe', 'mbe_0756', 'prompt_1070', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '51', '', 'At trial, Paul calls as his first witness, Stan, who was an eyewitness to the ac- cident. Stan proposes to testify that he heard Don tell Paul, "I know I was do- ing a wheelie." Don''s attorney objects. If Stan''s testimony is admitted, it will most likely be admitted because the proffered evidence is:', 'Admissible, as an opinion.', 'Admissible, as a party admis- sion.', 'Admissible, as a present sense impression.', 'Admissible, as a declaration against interest.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('757', 'mbe', 'mbe_0757', 'prompt_1071', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '53', '', 'Larry was charged with vehicular bat- tery after driving through a red light and crashing his Cadillac into Marie''s Lotus. Marie suffered massive internal injuries and lapsed into a coma until several hours after she reached the hospital. Sharon, Marie''s best friend, was a passenger in her car. Sharon mi- raculously received only a few minor bruises. After the collision, Sharon stayed with Marie, trying to comfort her until the ambulance arrived. At trial, Sharon is called to the stand by the district attorney, who asks Sharon if Marie said anything to her before being taken to the hospital. Sharon answers affirmatively, and testifies that Marie was conscious for a few brief moments after the accident when she had stated, "He never stopped for that red light." 324 EVIDENCE m 157 m Upon objection by Larry''s attorney, the court should rule the testimony con- cerning Marie''s statement to Sharon:', 'Inadmissible, because Marie''s statement is hearsay.', 'Inadmissible, because Marie''s statement lacks trustworthiness.', 'Admissible, as a present sense impression by Marie.', 'None of the above.', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('758', 'mbe', 'mbe_0758', 'prompt_1072', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '55', '', 'Fred and Wilma Strong had been mar- ried for thirty years. Late one Sunday afternoon, while on his way home from the pub, Fred hit a young child with his car and drove off. The young child died and, after months of police investiga- tion, Fred was charged with vehicular manslaughter. Thereafter, Fred called Attorney to set up a meeting, in order to retain him for the pending trial. At the consultation with Attorney, Fred was accompanied by Wilma. During the conference, Secretary took notes of the meeting. A month later, Secretary quit her job. Fred decided not to hire Attor- ney, but to act in pro per instead. Dur- ing Fred''s trial, the prosecution called Secretary to testify to what was said at the initial conference. The proposed testimony is:', 'Admissible, because Fred did not hire Attorney.', 'Admissible, because Secretary''s presence at the conference de- stroyed the privilege.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception.', 'Inadmissible, because it is opin- ion. EVIDENCE 325', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('759', 'mbe', 'mbe_0759', 'prompt_1073', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '56', '', 'In a battery action against Katz by Doug, Doug''s attorney called Mel to testify that the incident occurred on December 23. Although Mel was not questioned about a deposition he made before trial, Mel had previously testi- fied at his deposition that the incident occurred on December 14. After Mel''s trial testimony, Katz''s attorney offers Mel''s deposition into evidence. The trial judge should rule the deposition:', 'Admissible, as substantive evi- dence the incident occurred on December 14.', 'Inadmissible, because Mel was available to testify at the trial.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception.', 'Inadmissible, because there were no facts showing that Mel was intentionally untruthful. Questions 57-58 are based on the following facts: One morning, Don telephoned his best friend, Troy, and asked him if he could borrow his car. Don explained to Troy that he had an audit scheduled with the Internal Revenue Service, which he had to attend and he needed his car to get there. Don promised Troy that he would return the car late that afternoon. Troy agreed to allow Don to bor- row his car. While d', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('76', 'mbe', 'mbe_0076', 'prompt_0359', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '34', '', 'What if Fork had refused to sell the spoon to Van Gogh and the museum sues for breach of contract?', 'The museum would win because the delegation ofduty resulted in a third-party beneficiary con- tract, giving museum a cause of action.', 'Van Gogh would win because Fork owns the spoon.', 'Van Gogh will lose because there is no privity of contract.', 'Van Gogh will lose because the cause of action is against Whis- tler.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('760', 'mbe', 'mbe_0760', 'prompt_1074', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '57', '', 'In his case in chief, Bud calls Vic to testify to three incidents of careless driving on Don''s part. The trial judge should rule the testimony:', 'Admissible, as circumstantial evidence that Don was negligent on that occasion.', 'Admissible, since Vic had per- sonal knowledge of Don''s poor driving.', 'Admissible against Troy, as evi- dence of Don''s lack of fitness.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not within any exception.', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('761', 'mbe', 'mbe_0761', 'prompt_1075', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '58', '', 'In his defense, Troy offered testimony of his wife, Winnie, that Don had a reputation as a careful driver. Winnie''s testimony is: 326 EVIDENCE Q 5 mz C) m', 'Admissible, as evidence of habit.', 'Admissible, as present sense impression.', 'Admissible, because it is proper character evidence.', 'Inadmissible, because of bias.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('762', 'mbe', 'mbe_0762', 'prompt_1076', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'criminal', '60', '', 'Polly sues Marie, an artist, and Ben, an art gallery owner, for libel. Polly''s complaint alleges that Marie painted a picture portraying a figure recognized as Polly engaging in sexual conduct, and the painting was published by allowing the painting to be publicly displayed. Further, Ben also published the libelous painting by hanging the painting for public viewing in his art gallery and where it was viewed by the people visiting his gallery. At trial, Polly offers testimony of Witness that he was present in Ben''s gallery and ob- served Ben hanging the picture. Witness further offers to testify as to the scene portrayed in the picture and that the picture was signed with Marie''s name in the lower right-hand corner. Upon proper objection the court should rule Witness'' testimony:', 'Inadmissible, because Witness'' testimony is not the best evi- dence.', 'Inadmissible, because Witness'' description of the picture, as against Ben, is insufficiently authenticated.', 'Admissible, because Witness properly authenticated the paint- ing by identifying the artist who signed the painting as Marie. EVIDENCE 327 5 U.1', 'Admissible, because Witness has Questions 61 - 62 are based on the personal knowledge of the facts following facts: in which he is testifying to. Winona sues Bob for the wrongful death of her husband, Henry. At trial, Winona offers the testimony of Henry''s business partner, Paul, who states that the average net profit from the business of each partner over the preceding five years was $100,000. Fur- ther, evidence is presented showing that the partnership books are in Paul''s possession and that', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('763', 'mbe', 'mbe_0763', 'prompt_1077', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '62', '', 'Assume that Paul''s testimony is inad- missible. Winona now offers the testi- mony of a properly-qualified expert, Alvin, a certified public accountant, who states that he had reviewed the books of the partnership covering the preceding five years and, that in his opinion, the books reflect an average annual profit for that period allocable to Henry of $100,000. Upon objection, the court should rule Alvin''s testimony:', 'Admissible, because Alvin has personal knowledge of the earn- ings, and is a qualified expert.', 'Inadmissible, because of the best evidence rule.', 'Inadmissible, because Alvin does not have personal knowledge of the facts to which he is testify- ing.', 'Inadmissible, as hearsay not fall- ing within any exception.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('764', 'mbe', 'mbe_0764', 'prompt_1078', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '63', '', 'Greg dies, leaving a will by which he bequeaths his entire estate to his friend, Bobby. Peter, Greg''s only heir at law, brings a suit to contest the will on grounds of incapacity. At the trial, Bobby calls Dr. Jan and Mrs. Alice as witnesses, both of whom offer to testify affirmatively when asked whether Greg knew enough about "the nature and extent of his property, and the natural objects of his bounty" at the time of Greg''s execution of the will. Other evidence has shown: (1) that Mrs. Alice is Greg''s former housekeeper, who had seen him daily for over 25 years, until the date of Greg''s death; and (2) that Dr. Jan is a psychiatrist, stipulated by the parties to be qualified, who had occa- sion to examine Greg the week before Greg executed his will. Upon objection, the court should:', 'Exclude the testimony of both witnesses, because both are at- tempting to testify to their opin- ions on an ultimate issue in the case.', 'Admit Dr. Jan''s testimony, and exclude Mrs. Alice''s testimony.', 'Admit Mrs. Alice''s testimony, and exclude Dr. Jan''s testimo- ny.', 'Admit the testimony of both wit- nesses. EVIDENCE 329 Q 61ZWa 11''', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('765', 'mbe', 'mbe_0765', 'prompt_1079', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '64', '', 'Baby sued Dan for damages, for injuries that Baby incurred when a badly rotted limb fell from a tree in front of Dan''s home and hit Baby. Dan claimed that the tree was on city property; thus, was not liable to Baby for his injuries. At trial, Baby offers testimony that a week after the accident, Dan had the tree cut down. The evidence is:', 'Inadmissible, since such is against public policy so as to further safety precautions.', 'Inadmissible, since it is irrel- evant.', 'Admissible, to show that the tree was on Dan''s property.', 'Admissible to show that the tree was in rotten condition.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('766', 'mbe', 'mbe_0766', 'prompt_1080', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '65', '', 'Nancy was prosecuted for the murder of Merry. At trial, Anna testified against Nancy. On cross-examination, Nancy''s attorney asked Anna the following question, "Isn''t it true that charges against you as an accomplice in Merry''s murder are being dropped in exchange for your testimony against Nancy?" The question is:', 'Proper impeachment, because it shows Anna has a self-interest in testifying against Nancy.', 'Proper impeachment on cross- examination, even though ex- trinsic proof will not be allowed if the question is answered in the negative.', 'Improper impeachment.', 'Improper, because specific in- stances of misconduct may not be used to impeach.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('767', 'mbe', 'mbe_0767', 'prompt_1081', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '66', '', 'Terry and Larry were indicted for con- spiracy to sell an illegal substance. Dan, an undercover agent, was the key wit- ness. Terry and Larry claim entrapment as their defense. At trial, Dan testifies for the prosecution that he was present at a meeting with the defendants at the Grand Hotel on July 7, at which Terry and Larry agreed to sell two kilos of cocaine to Dan for $150,000. Thereafter, the defense calls as a wit- ness Tom, an old college roommate of Dan''s, to testify that Dan once purchased marijuana. Tom''s proposed testimony is:', 'Admissible, because the pur- chase of marijuana is probative of Dan''s untruthfulness.', 'Admissible, provided that the purchase of the marijuana re- sulted in a criminal conviction. 330 EVIDENCE m m C)', 'Inadmissible, because it is extrin- sic evidence.', 'Inadmissible, as improper lay opinion in regard to whether or not the substance previously pur- chased by Dan was marijuana.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('768', 'mbe', 'mbe_0768', 'prompt_1082', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '68', '', 'Denny was driving his Toyota truck when he struck Stan, a 6-year-old boy, who was crossing the street on the way to school. Stan was seriously injured. Stan visited attorney, Jacoby, accom- panied by his father, Tom, in regard to bringing a case against Denny for the injuries Stan sustained. Tom did not retain Jacoby to represent his son in the lawsuit. Instead, he hired another lawyer, Parker, to handle the case. At trial, Denny''s attorney calls Jacoby to testify what Stan had said to him in regard to his physical condition dur- ing the consultation that he (Jacoby) had with Stan and his father. Jacoby''s testimony is:', 'Sustain the objection, because Nurse is not a qualified expert.', 'Admissible, because the element of confidentiality is not satisfied when a third person is present with the attorney and client.', 'Inadmissible, because it was a statement of physical condi- tion.', 'Inadmissible, because of the at- torney-client privilege. EVIDENCE 331 Q ("3z LI I 5w', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('769', 'mbe', 'mbe_0769', 'prompt_1083', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '70', '', 'In a suit between Pamela and Darren, arising out of an automobile accident, Benny, a bystander, was called to the stand by Pamela''s counsel, to testify that Darren had driven through a red light, which caused the collision. On cross-examination, Benny is asked if he has ever been convicted for false pretenses, which Benny denies. The prosecutor offers a certified copy of the criminal conviction into evidence, upon Benny''s denial. Pamela''s counsel objects. The court should:', 'Grant the motion, on the grounds that the best evidence rule re- quires production of the news- paper.', 'Grant the motion, because the reference to the newspaper story does not fit within any exception to the hearsay rule.', 'Sustain the objection, because it is irrelevant.', 'Sustain the objection, because the witness'' character is not in issue.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('77', 'mbe', 'mbe_0077', 'prompt_0360', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 1', 'contracts', '35', '', 'What if Fork refuses to sell the spoon to Van Gogh and Van Gogh sues Whis- tler for Breach of Contract?', 'Van Gogh will lose because he assigned the contract.', 'Van Gogh will lose because of the doctrine of impossibility.', 'Van Gogh will win because Whistler remains the guarantor for the performance of Fork.', 'Van Gogh will win because Whistler''s duty was too personal to delegate. III CONTRACTS', 'D', '', '', 0, ''),
('770', 'mbe', 'mbe_0770', 'prompt_1084', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '71', '', 'Myron was arrested and charged with the rape of Patty. He claims he did not rape Patty because she had consented to his advances. At trial, Myron is prepared to testify that he had engaged in sexual intercourse with the victim on many occasions during the previous month before the rape allegedly occurred with the victim''s consent. Myron''s testimony is: 332 EVIDENCE Q', 'Admissible, as evidence of spe- cific acts to show consent.', 'Admissible, as evidence of habit.', 'Inadmissible character evi- dence.', 'Inadmissible, because reputa- tion or opinion evidence of past sexual behavior of the victim is not permitted.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('771', 'mbe', 'mbe_0771', 'prompt_1085', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '72', '', 'On January 3 1993, Dick was prosecuted for burglary. At trial, Dick testified on his own behalf, denying that he commit- ted the burglary. On cross-examination, the prosecution asked Dick if in 1980 he had been convicted of robbery. Dick''s attorney objects. The court should:', 'Overrule the objection, because the prosecutor may test the cred- ibility of the witness.', 'Admissible, because it is a party admission.', 'Inadmissible, because Eric''s statement is opinion.', 'Inadmissible, because the state- ment was made during an effort to settle a claim.', 'B', '', '', 0, ''),
('772', 'mbe', 'mbe_0772', 'prompt_1086', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '74', '', 'Bonnie was sued by Brent for the sale of her shares in the Acme Corporation. Bonnie consulted her friend Steve, an attorney, regarding the sale of her 80% interest of the stock of a closely held corporation. She explained the reason for the sale to Steve, and disclosed the financial condition of the corporation. Steve determined that the transaction was too complex and in violation of a shareholder agreement. Steve decided to decline the case. Bonnie then hired Carrie to represent her. EVIDENCE 333 z 5 At trial, Brent calls Steve to testify to the contents of the conversation be- tween Steve and Bonnie. Bonnie''s counsel objects. The court should:', 'Sustain the objection, because of the attorney-client relationship.', 'Sustain the objection, because the conversation is not relevant to the sale of the stock.', 'Overrule the objection, because Steve was not retained by Bon- nie.', 'Overrule the objection, because the attorney-client privilege did not attach.', 'C', '', '', 0, ''),
('773', 'mbe', 'mbe_0773', 'prompt_1087', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'evidence', '75', '', 'Mr. Roper owns a house at 24211 Lemon Street. He rents the house to Jack. The lease states that Jack will maintain the house and yard in its pres- ent condition. While walking home one day, Chrissie was hit on the head when a tree limb fell from Mr. Roper''s property, seriously injuring her. It is agreed between the parties that the tree fell because of dry rot. Mr. Roper hired Larry the next day to have the whole tree removed from the property. Chris- sie now brings suit against Mr. Roper in Superior Court. At trial, Mr. Roper testifies that he rented out the house to Jack, who he claims was responsible for the maintenance of the yard, under the terms of the lease. Chrissie introduces into evidence that Mr. Roper had Larry remove the tree from the property the next day. The evidence is:', 'Admissible, because the evi- dence is not being used to prove negligence or culpable conduct.', 'Admissible, because Mr. Roper was at fault.', 'Inadmissible, because Mr. Rop- er''s acts were subsequent reme- dial measures.', 'Inadmissible, because the pro- bative value substantially out- weighed its prejudicial effect. 334 EVIDENCE Q', 'A', '', '', 0, ''),
('774', 'mbe', 'mbe_0774', 'prompt_1088', 'Flemings''s Multistate Examination Workbook - Volume 2', 'contracts', '77', '', 'Botch was named as a criminal defen- dant for allegedly killing the cashier at a local convenience store. One of his defenses is that he is innocent by reason of a mental disease or defect such that he could not appreciate the wrongful- ness of his acts. The defense presented expert testimony through Dr. Wright who was properly qualified as an expert. Dr. Wright testified that Botch suffered a mental disease or defect at the time of his acts, as to the characteristics of that mental disease or defect, and his diagnosis of Botch. The defense then offered Dr. Wright''s opinion that Botch did not know his acts were wrong when committed, over objection of the district attorney. How will the court rule on the prosecution''s objection?', 'Overruled, because Dr. Wright was properly qualified as an expert before testifying.', 'Overruled, if Dr. Wright''s expert opinion that Botch suffered from a mental disease or defect was uncontroverted.', 'Sustained, because an expert cannot testify on whether the defendant had the mental condi- tion constituting an element of a defense to the crime charged.', 'Sustained, because Dr. Wright''s testimony was biased in favor of Botch. EVIDENCE 335 Questions 78 - 80 are based on the following facts: Yvette leaves Nordstrom without paying for a blouse. She is stopped after exiting and is charged with shoplifting. At trial, the pros- ecution introduces into evidence that Yvette exited from the department store without paying for the blouse, which was found on her baby stroller. Yvette testifies that she walked out of the store with the blouse hang- ing on th', 'C', '', '', 0, '')
